      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "34b169283010c8e7",
      "line_num": 335,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Audio/TTS UX polish (surfaced live in dev mode 2026-07-26; the last two ride with the Settings/Admin migration pass):**\n- [x] First-playback Kokoro load was silent for seconds \u2192 now a dismissible \"Loading the Kokoro voice model (first use)\u2026\" toast (`ResponseMessage.svelte`)\n- [x] Kokoro double-load in Settings (doubling progress %) \u2192 memoized load promise so `getVoices()` + the reactive share one download (`Settings/Audio.svelte`)\n- [ ] Opening Settings \u2192 Audio RELOADS Kokoro even after playback already loaded it \u2014 the Settings main-thread loader and the playback Web Worker (`kokoro.worker.ts`) are separate contexts; share one loader/model so the model loads once per session\n- [ ] Kokoro voice selector is janky (a type-in input + a list that only opens after you clear the field) \u2192 replace with a clean, DRY `<select>`/combobox\n\n`TODO.md:L335`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 5460
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 379,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Enforce the 8-char password server-side (`auths.py`) \u2014 measure-twice e2e verified (200\u2192bug, 400\u2192fixed); committed"
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 380,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Fix `UserUpdateForm` / `SpaceForm` TS\u2194Pydantic drift \u2014 committed"
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 381,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Delete the dead `SUPPORTED_FILE_EXTENSIONS` copy in `constants.ts` (backend 50-entry list is live) \u2014 committed"
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 382,
          "tags": [
            "#security",
            "#critical"
          ],
          "text": "**Reconcile the drifted permission-tree defaults** (2026-07-27; `make e2e` 13/15, both failures pre-existing leaks; committed). The backend described permissions twice \u2014 env-derived `DEFAULT_USER_PERMISSIONS`, which `has_permission()` ENFORCES, against Pydantic model defaults, which the admin panel ADVERTISES \u2014 with nothing keeping them in step. Diffed inside the image: **4 value disagreements** (the `sharing.public_*` four, latent because persisted values masked them) and **1 key missing entirely**. `features.web_search` was live and user-visible \u2014 the panel advertised it on, enforcement read the absent key as denied, so **no non-admin ever got the web-search button**. Fixes: added `USER_PERMISSIONS_FEATURES_WEB_SEARCH` (default `True`); the Pydantic defaults now DERIVE from the table, so a field with no entry raises `KeyError` at import rather than shipping a silent policy lie; `get_permissions()` backfills, so upgraded deployments get the key too. `permissions-drift.cy.ts` asserts the observable contract rather than the internal tables, and is deliberately one-directional \u2014 a v1.1.1 database still carries the retired `workspace` group. Measure-twice RED (2 of 3 failing) then GREEN, and verified against a real v1.1.1 prod snapshot via `KEEP=1 make upgrade_gate` (20/21)."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 383,
          "tags": [
            "#frontend",
            "#tech-debt"
          ],
          "text": "**Drop the frontend's permission fallbacks** (follow-up to the reconcile above): the 13 `$user?.permissions?.x?.y ?? true` sites in `src/` all match the backend today, and now that every enforced key is guaranteed present in the payload they are unreachable defaults. Worse, they can only ever mask future drift \u2014 a `?? true` renders a control the API will refuse. Delete them so the frontend reads what the backend sent, with a guard-rail that a denied permission hides its control."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 384,
          "tags": [
            "#critical"
          ],
          "text": "**RESOLVED \u2014 never a product bug: `| grep -q` under `set -o pipefail`** (2026-07-28; `gauntlet_full` exit 0, `upgrade_gate` **21/21**). `writer | grep -q PATTERN` reports a MATCH as a FAILURE: grep exits on the first hit, the writer still has output queued, takes SIGPIPE and dies 141, and pipefail surfaces 141 as the pipeline's status. It only fires when the writer had more to say, which is why it read as flakiness. Reproduced deterministically (200k-line writer \u2192 141 on a match, five runs of five). It cost three gates, and the third is the bad one: `upgrade-gate.sh:55` asserts the ABSENCE of a traceback, where 141 reads as \"no match\" \u2014 so that check reported clean on exactly the failure it exists to catch. Devices, in preference order: pipe-free primitives in `scripts/lib/gate.sh` (`text_has`, `log_has`, `wait_for_log`, `fetch_has`) that capture the text and match with bash's own operators; every unbounded-writer site converted (12); `make pipefail_lint` refuses the shape returning, verified to fail on a reintroduced line; and `make pipefail_fixture` proves BOTH halves, because a device that fixes nothing looks identical to one that works unless the broken shape is asserted too. Scope is deliberate and written into the lint \u2014 only `docker logs` and `curl` writers are banned, since `printf`/`echo` are builtins that finish before grep can exit."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 385,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Cruft sweep (deps): removed unused `chart.js` + `@mediapipe/tasks-vision` from package.json (zero code refs \u2014 grep + a clean vite build both confirm) and the dead `DOMPurify` import in `MarkdownInlineTokens.svelte`. bun.lock reconciled (`Removed: 2`); `it_build` frozen-install + build green; markdown/sanitizer guard-rails green. (A fuller depcheck of the other 100+ deps is a separate pass.) Committed."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 386,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Strip hyperscript attribute forms (`_`, `script`, `data-script`) in the user-content sanitizer \u2014 one app-wide DOMPurify `uponSanitizeAttribute` hook (`utils/sanitize.ts`, installed in root `+layout.svelte`) covers all 14 `@html` sites; `data-script` survived DOMPurify's default `ALLOW_DATA_ATTR`, the other two were already dropped. Defense-in-depth (hyperscript not yet loaded). Guard-rail `content-security-sanitizer.cy.ts` measure-twice: RED pre-fix (data-script leaked via a rendered banner), GREEN post. Committed."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 387,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Perf: `Cache-Control: immutable` on `_app/immutable/*` \u2014 measure-twice e2e verified (empty header\u2192immutable); committed"
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 388,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Perf: eager ML libs off the conversation chunk (nodes/2) \u2014 MEASURED: the 22 MB WASM was already lazy (feature-gated, NOT on the load path; the audit inferred wrong). Real win: deleted dead `@huggingface/transformers` import + made kokoro-js dynamic in `chat/Settings/Audio.svelte`. Fresh-build verified: nodes/2 2.70\u21920.65 MB, app load 5.39\u21924.12 MB transfer (\u221224%), 13.4\u219210.6 MB decoded; auth+cache guard-rails green. Committed."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 389,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Perf: `/icons/` waste \u2014 RESOLVED as already-gated. Image ships 0 `.xcf` (`.dockerignore **/*.xcf`) and 1.3 MB icons total; the audit read the stale host build. The 6 identical 170 KB PNGs are placeholder art meant to diverge (favicon/logo/splash + dark variants) \u2192 branding-art task, not perf."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 390,
          "tags": [
            "#perf",
            "#frontend"
          ],
          "text": "Perf/arch: lazy-load markdown-render libs (katex/mermaid/highlight/codemirror) \u2014 SHIPPED as architecture hardening, MEASURED as perf-neutral. Four changes landed: (A) deleted the DEAD `import katex` in `utils/marked/katex-extension.ts` (tokenizer never used it; real render is `KatexRenderer.svelte`, untouched \u2192 tex still renders); (B) `hljs` now dynamic-imported inside `copyToClipboard` (copy-only, off every page's utils chunk); (C+D) `CodeBlock` + `KatexRenderer` load via memoized `{#await import()}` loaders (`Markdown/lazy.ts`) only when a code/math token renders \u2014 streaming-safe (one promise identity, no re-pending flash). MEASURE-TWICE FINDING: the hoped-for \"plain convo sheds ~10 MB\" is NOT real \u2014 mermaid/codemirror/katex were ALREADY lazy (Vite component chunking); a plain convo never pulled them, before or after (measured: release-3.0.0 vs develop, identical 13.41\u219210.27 delta = the nodes/2 win alone; no separate mermaid/codemirror chunk fetched in EITHER build). So this change's value is real but not bytes: dead-code removal, hljs deferral, and EXPLICIT/structural laziness aligned with the plan's islands principle (robust vs a future refactor making them eager). Guard-rail `markdown-render-lazy.cy.ts` GREEN before AND after (seeds a chat, asserts `.katex` + `.copy-code-button` + `pre.mermaid` render; plain-text control asserts none) \u2014 proves the refactor broke no rendering."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 391,
          "tags": [
            "#perf",
            "#frontend"
          ],
          "text": "Perf: batch the serial boot waterfall into `Promise.all` \u2014 the `(app)/+layout.svelte` boot ladder awaited `getModels` \u2192 `getBanners` \u2192 `getTools` \u2192 `getToolServersData` one at a time, though only the first depends on anything (user settings, for `directConnections`). Now one wave after settings. MEASURED (fresh container, `boot-waterfall-timing.json`): BEFORE the three were strictly disjoint \u2014 models `272.7\u21921041.3`, banners `1043.9\u21921050.2`, tools `1052.8\u21921060.3`; AFTER all three start within 1 ms of each other and banners/tools finish well inside the models window. Local saving is small in absolute terms (~19 ms, since banners/tools are ~7 ms each on loopback) but it is three round-trips removed, so the win scales with RTT \u2014 on a 60 ms link that is ~180 ms off first paint. Guard-rail `boot-waterfall.cy.ts` measure-twice: RED pre-change (last start 1153.1 ms vs first end 1139.7 ms \u2014 no overlap), GREEN after. The spec also pins the dependency that must SURVIVE: settings still resolves before `/api/models` is issued. Committed."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 392,
          "tags": [
            "#perf"
          ],
          "text": "**Perf: caching pass** (2026-07-26; `make e2e` 12/14, only the two known leaks failing; committed). Surveyed every layer that could take a cache, fixed the two that paid. Framing first, because it decides the rest: the \"$5 VPS serves 100k concurrent\" pattern caches *anonymous identical HTML*, and this app has almost none \u2014 nearly every response is per-user authenticated. **(1) Base-models cache.** `/api/models` probed every provider on every call: measured live, **~740 ms uncached against ~4.5 ms cached (~160\u00d7)**, with `ENABLE_BASE_MODELS_CACHE` defaulting to `False`. Flipped the default, which also engages the existing startup warm. It had been off because the cache has no TTL and nothing cleared it, so `invalidate_base_models_cache()` now fires from both provider-config update endpoints. `models-cache.cy.ts` asserts both halves, because a fast cache with broken invalidation is worse than the slow path; RED at 749 ms before, green after. **(2) `/static` and `/assets` shipped no `Cache-Control`** \u2014 ETag only, so every repeat visit spent a revalidation round-trip. `CachedStaticFiles` sets a BOUNDED `public, max-age=604800`, deliberately not `immutable`, because those filenames are reused across deploys. **Combined, same harness: boot data-fetch span 821.5 \u2192 102.5 ms (8\u00d7), `/api/models` 768 \u2192 17 ms.** Upgrade caveat: `ENABLE_BASE_MODELS_CACHE` is a `PersistentConfig`, so an admin who explicitly saved it keeps their choice."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 393,
          "tags": [
            "#perf",
            "#tech-debt"
          ],
          "text": "**`@cached(ttl=MODELS_CACHE_TTL)` never hits \u2014 the decorator is inert** (found during the caching pass, 2026-07-26): `routers/openai.py:550` and `routers/ollama.py:378` use bare `aiocache.cached` with no `key_builder`. aiocache's default key is built from `str(args)`, and the first arg is a Starlette `Request` \u2014 a new object with a new address on every call \u2014 so the key never repeats. PROVEN: with `MODELS_CACHE_TTL=60`, five back-to-back `/api/models` calls took 0.74 / 0.73 / 0.78 / 1.70 / 0.74 s. Zero hits. Two consequences: `MODELS_CACHE_TTL` is a knob that does nothing, and every request stores an entry held for the TTL, so memory grows with request rate. Fix is a `key_builder` keyed on stable values (user id + provider-config revision) \u2014 or delete the decorators, since removing something that never hits is behaviour-preserving. Same bare-`cached` import also appears in `utils/chat.py`, `utils/middleware.py`, and `utils/models.py`; audit those too."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 394,
          "tags": [
            "#perf",
            "#architecture"
          ],
          "text": "**Redis/valkey + multi-worker \u2014 the actual concurrency lever** (scoped during the caching pass, needs its own plan): `start.sh:233` runs `--workers ${UVICORN_WORKERS:-1}`, so this is a SINGLE-process app today. `REDIS_URL`, `WEBSOCKET_MANAGER`, `WEBSOCKET_REDIS_URL` and a redis-backed `PersistentConfig` all already exist in the codebase, but nothing in the Dockerfile, compose, or CapRover config sets them. Until they are wired, raising `UVICORN_WORKERS` breaks websocket fan-out (each worker holds its own socket state) and duplicates every in-process cache per worker. This \u2014 not HTML caching \u2014 is the memcached-shaped hole in the Django comparison. Needs: a plan, a valkey sidecar decision (in-image vs CapRover one-click vs Sprig\u2122), and a multi-worker test that proves socket events cross workers."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 395,
          "tags": [
            "#perf"
          ],
          "text": "Perf: put HTTP/2 in front (the other half of the old waterfall item \u2014 parallel boot fetches pay off more once they stop queueing behind HTTP/1.1 connection limits)"
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 396,
          "tags": [
            "#perf"
          ],
          "text": "Perf: `/api/models` alone is ~770\u2013995 ms of a ~820\u20131100 ms boot. INVESTIGATED 2026-07-26, and it is **latency, not payload** \u2014 that measurement was taken with an EMPTY model list, so there is nothing to paginate. The time is spent in `get_all_base_models` (`utils/models.py:60-76`) probing the OpenAI and Ollama providers; it already runs them under one `asyncio.gather`, so the cost is the slowest single probe (a connect timeout in the zero-egress e2e container). Worse, **`ENABLE_BASE_MODELS_CACHE` defaults to `False`** (`config.py:994-997`), so every call re-probes every provider \u2014 on a real deployment that is an upstream round-trip per boot, per refresh, per user. Two separate levers, each wanting its own guard-rail and before/after: (a) **backend** \u2014 default the base-models cache on and/or warm it at startup, and bound the per-provider probe timeout; (b) **frontend/islands** \u2014 stop gating `loaded` on it: render the shell, fill the selector when it lands, and make the admin setup-wizard check (which reads `$models.length === 0`) await the promise instead of the boot chain. Pagination is a THIRD, separate lever that only pays off once a payload is measured large on a loaded instance \u2014 same open question for the chat list, tools, prompts, and knowledge, which grow with tenant size."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 397,
          "tags": [
            "#e2e"
          ],
          "text": "**Poka-Yoke pass on the e2e suite \u2014 `make e2e` GREEN, 15/15 specs, 30/30 tests** (2026-07-27). Three devices, each aimed at the CLASS rather than the instance. **(1) Shared-container config leaks.** The harness boots ONE container per run, so anything a spec writes to server config silently changes the world for every spec after it. It bit twice \u2014 `stt-misconfig` left `STT_ENGINE=openai` behind, and a banner seeded by `boot-waterfall` sat on later specs' pages \u2014 and a defect that happens twice is the trigger to change the system. `support/e2e.ts` now snapshots the admin-mutable config surface before each spec and restores ONLY what changed, so specs that touch nothing issue zero writes and a leak is logged BY NAME rather than surfacing as whichever spec fails next. **(2) Implicit precondition.** `stt-not-configured` asserted behaviour for \"no STT engine\" while merely inheriting whatever the container held; it sets `stt.ENGINE=''` itself now, and is true in any order. **(3) The sprigs-panel failure was NOT a leak \u2014 my original diagnosis was wrong.** The graft succeeded; `cy.contains('Restart the Rootstock')` matched nothing because grafting became restart-free and the note was reworded, so a genuine UX improvement surfaced as a failing graft test. The assertion now reads `post_graft_note` from `/catalog`, and the test is pinned `retries: 0` \u2014 a retry of a mutating test is not a retry, it is a different test against different state."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 398,
          "tags": [
            "#e2e",
            "#critical"
          ],
          "text": "**The e2e harness could silently run on the PREVIOUS run's data** (2026-07-29). `run-cypress.sh` did `docker rm -f \"$ROOT\"` then `docker volume rm \"$VOL\" ... || true`, and that `|| true` was the whole bug: a container still going away holds its volume, the removal fails, and the run boots on the last run's data **with nothing printed and no non-zero status anywhere**. Third instance of one class \u2014 *a gate whose failure is indistinguishable from success* \u2014 after `docker run` without `-i` and `| grep -q` under pipefail. The symptom is the dangerous part: it surfaced as `sprigs-panel` failing on a surface nobody had touched, which sends the diagnosis to the wrong place and reads as a flake. Fix: wait for the container to actually be gone, retry the volume removal, and hard-exit naming the container still holding it. Proved able to fire by pinning the volume with an unrelated container \u2014 `FATAL: volume 'sage-e2e-data' survived removal`. Process note: do not run docker experiments beside a running gate."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 399,
          "tags": [
            "#e2e",
            "#frontend"
          ],
          "text": "**\"Would Selenium test the colour picker?\" \u2014 no, and the direction I called human-only never was** (Alexander, 2026-07-29). No WebDriver-family tool can open the OS colour dialog, because the browser renders it outside the page. But that dialog is not our code. Our contract is \"when the picker's value changes, the hex field follows\", and a synthetic `input` event exercises exactly that \u2014 `color-pair.js` is delegated from the document and Svelte's `bind:value` reacts to the same event. Tested green against BOTH implementations and proved able to fail by breaking only the picker\u2192hex branch. I had told Alexander this needed a human; that was overstated, and it is fixed rather than left as a standing manual step. **Where image-based automation (SikuliX) earns its place** is real input physics: Phase S shipped an autoscroll that went 13/13 green under a real driver and was broken on a real trackpad. Keep it on the roadmap for the streaming and scroll surfaces (Phases 3\u20134); it is close to worthless for form surfaces, where a synthetic event reaches the same code the human does."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 400,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Guard-rail Cypress spec for every area before it's touched"
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 401,
          "tags": [
            "#e2e",
            "#tech-debt"
          ],
          "text": "Modernize (or prune) the inherited upstream e2e specs \u2014 `registration/settings/chat/documents.cy.ts` now COMPILE (import depth `../support`\u2192`../../support` fixed) but FAIL on stale open-webui UI: they assume OPEN signup (this fork hard-closes it after the first admin, so the \"Sign up\" toggle is hidden) and the old changelog dismiss `Okay, Let's Go!` (now the setup wizard's \"Get Started\"/Close, handled via API). Rewrite to the fork's actual flows or drop tests of removed behavior. Note: they're in a subdir so NOT in the default `make e2e` gate."
        }
      ],
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "b76eee3a936cebd3",
      "line_num": 378,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Phase Q \u2014 clear the decks: current-SPA drift fixes, cruft, and perf quick-wins (no migration needed).**\n- [x] Enforce the 8-char password server-side (`auths.py`) \u2014 measure-twice e2e verified (200\u2192bug, 400\u2192fixed); committed\n- [x] Fix `UserUpdateForm` / `SpaceForm` TS\u2194Pydantic drift \u2014 committed\n- [x] Delete the dead `SUPPORTED_FILE_EXTENSIONS` copy in `constants.ts` (backend 50-entry list is live) \u2014 committed\n- [x] **Reconcile the drifted permission-tree defaults** (2026-07-27; `make e2e` 13/15, both failures pre-existing leaks; committed). The backend described permissions twice \u2014 env-derived `DEFAULT_USER_PERMISSIONS`, which `has_permission()` ENFORCES, against Pydantic model defaults, which the admin panel ADVERTISES \u2014 with nothing keeping them in step. Diffed inside the image: **4 value disagreements** (the `sharing.public_*` four, latent because persisted values masked them) and **1 key missing entirely**. `features.web_search` was live and user-visible \u2014 the panel advertised it on, enforcement read the absent key as denied, so **no non-admin ever got the web-search button**. Fixes: added `USER_PERMISSIONS_FEATURES_WEB_SEARCH` (default `True`); the Pydantic defaults now DERIVE from the table, so a field with no entry raises `KeyError` at import rather than shipping a silent policy lie; `get_permissions()` backfills, so upgraded deployments get the key too. `permissions-drift.cy.ts` asserts the observable contract rather than the internal tables, and is deliberately one-directional \u2014 a v1.1.1 database still carries the retired `workspace` group. Measure-twice RED (2 of 3 failing) then GREEN, and verified against a real v1.1.1 prod snapshot via `KEEP=1 make upgrade_gate` (20/21). #security #critical\n- [ ] **Drop the frontend's permission fallbacks** (follow-up to the reconcile above): the 13 `$user?.permissions?.x?.y ?? true` sites in `src/` all match the backend today, and now that every enforced key is guaranteed present in the payload they are unreachable defaults. Worse, they can only ever mask future drift \u2014 a `?? true` renders a control the API will refuse. Delete them so the frontend reads what the backend sent, with a guard-rail that a denied permission hides its control. #frontend #tech-debt\n- [x] **RESOLVED \u2014 never a product bug: `| grep -q` under `set -o pipefail`** (2026-07-28; `gauntlet_full` exit 0, `upgrade_gate` **21/21**). `writer | grep -q PATTERN` reports a MATCH as a FAILURE: grep exits on the first hit, the writer still has output queued, takes SIGPIPE and dies 141, and pipefail surfaces 141 as the pipeline's status. It only fires when the writer had more to say, which is why it read as flakiness. Reproduced deterministically (200k-line writer \u2192 141 on a match, five runs of five). It cost three gates, and the third is the bad one: `upgrade-gate.sh:55` asserts the ABSENCE of a traceback, where 141 reads as \"no match\" \u2014 so that check reported clean on exactly the failure it exists to catch. Devices, in preference order: pipe-free primitives in `scripts/lib/gate.sh` (`text_has`, `log_has`, `wait_for_log`, `fetch_has`) that capture the text and match with bash's own operators; every unbounded-writer site converted (12); `make pipefail_lint` refuses the shape returning, verified to fail on a reintroduced line; and `make pipefail_fixture` proves BOTH halves, because a device that fixes nothing looks identical to one that works unless the broken shape is asserted too. Scope is deliberate and written into the lint \u2014 only `docker logs` and `curl` writers are banned, since `printf`/`echo` are builtins that finish before grep can exit. #critical\n- [x] Cruft sweep (deps): removed unused `chart.js` + `@mediapipe/tasks-vision` from package.json (zero code refs \u2014 grep + a clean vite build both confirm) and the dead `DOMPurify` import in `MarkdownInlineTokens.svelte`. bun.lock reconciled (`Removed: 2`); `it_build` frozen-install + build green; markdown/sanitizer guard-rails green. (A fuller depcheck of the other 100+ deps is a separate pass.) Committed.\n- [x] Strip hyperscript attribute forms (`_`, `script`, `data-script`) in the user-content sanitizer \u2014 one app-wide DOMPurify `uponSanitizeAttribute` hook (`utils/sanitize.ts`, installed in root `+layout.svelte`) covers all 14 `@html` sites; `data-script` survived DOMPurify's default `ALLOW_DATA_ATTR`, the other two were already dropped. Defense-in-depth (hyperscript not yet loaded). Guard-rail `content-security-sanitizer.cy.ts` measure-twice: RED pre-fix (data-script leaked via a rendered banner), GREEN post. Committed.\n- [x] Perf: `Cache-Control: immutable` on `_app/immutable/*` \u2014 measure-twice e2e verified (empty header\u2192immutable); committed\n- [x] Perf: eager ML libs off the conversation chunk (nodes/2) \u2014 MEASURED: the 22 MB WASM was already lazy (feature-gated, NOT on the load path; the audit inferred wrong). Real win: deleted dead `@huggingface/transformers` import + made kokoro-js dynamic in `chat/Settings/Audio.svelte`. Fresh-build verified: nodes/2 2.70\u21920.65 MB, app load 5.39\u21924.12 MB transfer (\u221224%), 13.4\u219210.6 MB decoded; auth+cache guard-rails green. Committed.\n- [x] Perf: `/icons/` waste \u2014 RESOLVED as already-gated. Image ships 0 `.xcf` (`.dockerignore **/*.xcf`) and 1.3 MB icons total; the audit read the stale host build. The 6 identical 170 KB PNGs are placeholder art meant to diverge (favicon/logo/splash + dark variants) \u2192 branding-art task, not perf.\n- [x] Perf/arch: lazy-load markdown-render libs (katex/mermaid/highlight/codemirror) \u2014 SHIPPED as architecture hardening, MEASURED as perf-neutral. Four changes landed: (A) deleted the DEAD `import katex` in `utils/marked/katex-extension.ts` (tokenizer never used it; real render is `KatexRenderer.svelte`, untouched \u2192 tex still renders); (B) `hljs` now dynamic-imported inside `copyToClipboard` (copy-only, off every page's utils chunk); (C+D) `CodeBlock` + `KatexRenderer` load via memoized `{#await import()}` loaders (`Markdown/lazy.ts`) only when a code/math token renders \u2014 streaming-safe (one promise identity, no re-pending flash). MEASURE-TWICE FINDING: the hoped-for \"plain convo sheds ~10 MB\" is NOT real \u2014 mermaid/codemirror/katex were ALREADY lazy (Vite component chunking); a plain convo never pulled them, before or after (measured: release-3.0.0 vs develop, identical 13.41\u219210.27 delta = the nodes/2 win alone; no separate mermaid/codemirror chunk fetched in EITHER build). So this change's value is real but not bytes: dead-code removal, hljs deferral, and EXPLICIT/structural laziness aligned with the plan's islands principle (robust vs a future refactor making them eager). Guard-rail `markdown-render-lazy.cy.ts` GREEN before AND after (seeds a chat, asserts `.katex` + `.copy-code-button` + `pre.mermaid` render; plain-text control asserts none) \u2014 proves the refactor broke no rendering. #perf #frontend\n- [x] Perf: batch the serial boot waterfall into `Promise.all` \u2014 the `(app)/+layout.svelte` boot ladder awaited `getModels` \u2192 `getBanners` \u2192 `getTools` \u2192 `getToolServersData` one at a time, though only the first depends on anything (user settings, for `directConnections`). Now one wave after settings. MEASURED (fresh container, `boot-waterfall-timing.json`): BEFORE the three were strictly disjoint \u2014 models `272.7\u21921041.3`, banners `1043.9\u21921050.2`, tools `1052.8\u21921060.3`; AFTER all three start within 1 ms of each other and banners/tools finish well inside the models window. Local saving is small in absolute terms (~19 ms, since banners/tools are ~7 ms each on loopback) but it is three round-trips removed, so the win scales with RTT \u2014 on a 60 ms link that is ~180 ms off first paint. Guard-rail `boot-waterfall.cy.ts` measure-twice: RED pre-change (last start 1153.1 ms vs first end 1139.7 ms \u2014 no overlap), GREEN after. The spec also pins the dependency that must SURVIVE: settings still resolves before `/api/models` is issued. Committed. #perf #frontend\n- [x] **Perf: caching pass** (2026-07-26; `make e2e` 12/14, only the two known leaks failing; committed). Surveyed every layer that could take a cache, fixed the two that paid. Framing first, because it decides the rest: the \"$5 VPS serves 100k concurrent\" pattern caches *anonymous identical HTML*, and this app has almost none \u2014 nearly every response is per-user authenticated. **(1) Base-models cache.** `/api/models` probed every provider on every call: measured live, **~740 ms uncached against ~4.5 ms cached (~160\u00d7)**, with `ENABLE_BASE_MODELS_CACHE` defaulting to `False`. Flipped the default, which also engages the existing startup warm. It had been off because the cache has no TTL and nothing cleared it, so `invalidate_base_models_cache()` now fires from both provider-config update endpoints. `models-cache.cy.ts` asserts both halves, because a fast cache with broken invalidation is worse than the slow path; RED at 749 ms before, green after. **(2) `/static` and `/assets` shipped no `Cache-Control`** \u2014 ETag only, so every repeat visit spent a revalidation round-trip. `CachedStaticFiles` sets a BOUNDED `public, max-age=604800`, deliberately not `immutable`, because those filenames are reused across deploys. **Combined, same harness: boot data-fetch span 821.5 \u2192 102.5 ms (8\u00d7), `/api/models` 768 \u2192 17 ms.** Upgrade caveat: `ENABLE_BASE_MODELS_CACHE` is a `PersistentConfig`, so an admin who explicitly saved it keeps their choice. #perf\n- [ ] **`@cached(ttl=MODELS_CACHE_TTL)` never hits \u2014 the decorator is inert** (found during the caching pass, 2026-07-26): `routers/openai.py:550` and `routers/ollama.py:378` use bare `aiocache.cached` with no `key_builder`. aiocache's default key is built from `str(args)`, and the first arg is a Starlette `Request` \u2014 a new object with a new address on every call \u2014 so the key never repeats. PROVEN: with `MODELS_CACHE_TTL=60`, five back-to-back `/api/models` calls took 0.74 / 0.73 / 0.78 / 1.70 / 0.74 s. Zero hits. Two consequences: `MODELS_CACHE_TTL` is a knob that does nothing, and every request stores an entry held for the TTL, so memory grows with request rate. Fix is a `key_builder` keyed on stable values (user id + provider-config revision) \u2014 or delete the decorators, since removing something that never hits is behaviour-preserving. Same bare-`cached` import also appears in `utils/chat.py`, `utils/middleware.py`, and `utils/models.py`; audit those too. #perf #tech-debt\n- [ ] **Redis/valkey + multi-worker \u2014 the actual concurrency lever** (scoped during the caching pass, needs its own plan): `start.sh:233` runs `--workers ${UVICORN_WORKERS:-1}`, so this is a SINGLE-process app today. `REDIS_URL`, `WEBSOCKET_MANAGER`, `WEBSOCKET_REDIS_URL` and a redis-backed `PersistentConfig` all already exist in the codebase, but nothing in the Dockerfile, compose, or CapRover config sets them. Until they are wired, raising `UVICORN_WORKERS` breaks websocket fan-out (each worker holds its own socket state) and duplicates every in-process cache per worker. This \u2014 not HTML caching \u2014 is the memcached-shaped hole in the Django comparison. Needs: a plan, a valkey sidecar decision (in-image vs CapRover one-click vs Sprig\u2122), and a multi-worker test that proves socket events cross workers. #perf #architecture\n- [ ] Perf: put HTTP/2 in front (the other half of the old waterfall item \u2014 parallel boot fetches pay off more once they stop queueing behind HTTP/1.1 connection limits) #perf\n- [ ] Perf: `/api/models` alone is ~770\u2013995 ms of a ~820\u20131100 ms boot. INVESTIGATED 2026-07-26, and it is **latency, not payload** \u2014 that measurement was taken with an EMPTY model list, so there is nothing to paginate. The time is spent in `get_all_base_models` (`utils/models.py:60-76`) probing the OpenAI and Ollama providers; it already runs them under one `asyncio.gather`, so the cost is the slowest single probe (a connect timeout in the zero-egress e2e container). Worse, **`ENABLE_BASE_MODELS_CACHE` defaults to `False`** (`config.py:994-997`), so every call re-probes every provider \u2014 on a real deployment that is an upstream round-trip per boot, per refresh, per user. Two separate levers, each wanting its own guard-rail and before/after: (a) **backend** \u2014 default the base-models cache on and/or warm it at startup, and bound the per-provider probe timeout; (b) **frontend/islands** \u2014 stop gating `loaded` on it: render the shell, fill the selector when it lands, and make the admin setup-wizard check (which reads `$models.length === 0`) await the promise instead of the boot chain. Pagination is a THIRD, separate lever that only pays off once a payload is measured large on a loaded instance \u2014 same open question for the chat list, tools, prompts, and knowledge, which grow with tenant size. #perf\n- [x] **Poka-Yoke pass on the e2e suite \u2014 `make e2e` GREEN, 15/15 specs, 30/30 tests** (2026-07-27). Three devices, each aimed at the CLASS rather than the instance. **(1) Shared-container config leaks.** The harness boots ONE container per run, so anything a spec writes to server config silently changes the world for every spec after it. It bit twice \u2014 `stt-misconfig` left `STT_ENGINE=openai` behind, and a banner seeded by `boot-waterfall` sat on later specs' pages \u2014 and a defect that happens twice is the trigger to change the system. `support/e2e.ts` now snapshots the admin-mutable config surface before each spec and restores ONLY what changed, so specs that touch nothing issue zero writes and a leak is logged BY NAME rather than surfacing as whichever spec fails next. **(2) Implicit precondition.** `stt-not-configured` asserted behaviour for \"no STT engine\" while merely inheriting whatever the container held; it sets `stt.ENGINE=''` itself now, and is true in any order. **(3) The sprigs-panel failure was NOT a leak \u2014 my original diagnosis was wrong.** The graft succeeded; `cy.contains('Restart the Rootstock')` matched nothing because grafting became restart-free and the note was reworded, so a genuine UX improvement surfaced as a failing graft test. The assertion now reads `post_graft_note` from `/catalog`, and the test is pinned `retries: 0` \u2014 a retry of a mutating test is not a retry, it is a different test against different state. #e2e\n- [x] **The e2e harness could silently run on the PREVIOUS run's data** (2026-07-29). `run-cypress.sh` did `docker rm -f \"$ROOT\"` then `docker volume rm \"$VOL\" ... || true`, and that `|| true` was the whole bug: a container still going away holds its volume, the removal fails, and the run boots on the last run's data **with nothing printed and no non-zero status anywhere**. Third instance of one class \u2014 *a gate whose failure is indistinguishable from success* \u2014 after `docker run` without `-i` and `| grep -q` under pipefail. The symptom is the dangerous part: it surfaced as `sprigs-panel` failing on a surface nobody had touched, which sends the diagnosis to the wrong place and reads as a flake. Fix: wait for the container to actually be gone, retry the volume removal, and hard-exit naming the container still holding it. Proved able to fire by pinning the volume with an unrelated container \u2014 `FATAL: volume 'sage-e2e-data' survived removal`. Process note: do not run docker experiments beside a running gate. #e2e #critical\n- [x] **\"Would Selenium test the colour picker?\" \u2014 no, and the direction I called human-only never was** (Alexander, 2026-07-29). No WebDriver-family tool can open the OS colour dialog, because the browser renders it outside the page. But that dialog is not our code. Our contract is \"when the picker's value changes, the hex field follows\", and a synthetic `input` event exercises exactly that \u2014 `color-pair.js` is delegated from the document and Svelte's `bind:value` reacts to the same event. Tested green against BOTH implementations and proved able to fail by breaking only the picker\u2192hex branch. I had told Alexander this needed a human; that was overstated, and it is fixed rather than left as a standing manual step. **Where image-based automation (SikuliX) earns its place** is real input physics: Phase S shipped an autoscroll that went 13/13 green under a real driver and was broken on a real trackpad. Keep it on the roadmap for the streaming and scroll surfaces (Phases 3\u20134); it is close to worthless for form surfaces, where a synthetic event reaches the same code the human does. #e2e #frontend\n- [ ] Guard-rail Cypress spec for every area before it's touched\n- [ ] Modernize (or prune) the inherited upstream e2e specs \u2014 `registration/settings/chat/documents.cy.ts` now COMPILE (import depth `../support`\u2192`../../support` fixed) but FAIL on stale open-webui UI: they assume OPEN signup (this fork hard-closes it after the first admin, so the \"Sign up\" toggle is hidden) and the old changelog dismiss `Okay, Let's Go!` (now the setup wizard's \"Get Started\"/Close, handled via API). Rewrite to the fork's actual flows or drop tests of removed behavior. Note: they're in a subdir so NOT in the default `make e2e` gate. #e2e #tech-debt\n\n`TODO.md:L378`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 5550
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "73f7fe99e7092666",
      "line_num": 423,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#frontend",
        "#ux"
      ],
      "text": "**Human UX review of the no-build surfaces before any of them takes over a route (Alexander, 2026-07-28: \"it's close but the UX feels a little off\"). Two surfaces exist in both forms right now \u2014 `/admin/sprigs` vs `/pages/admin/sprigs`, `/admin/diagnostics` vs `/pages/admin/diagnostics` \u2014 which makes this the cheapest it will ever be to judge: same data, same deployment, flip between them. `scripts/manual-check.sh` boots a seeded instance with both live (`--graft-ui` for the marketplace slot). This is not a nice-to-have: Phase S established that a green suite is the WEAKEST evidence on an interactive surface (an autoscroll went 13/13 green and was broken on a real trackpad), and this phase has already produced a second instance of the same thing \u2014 the \"Show me how to fix this\" button rendered and did nothing, every automated assertion passed, and a human click found it in seconds. Known feel-differences to judge rather than assume: messages are CSS toasts fixed to the top instead of a toast library; the fix remedy is a `<details>` disclosure instead of a modal; the diagnostics timestamp is absolute instead of a live relative label; the Sprigs panel swaps whole rather than patching; the branding preview reflects SAVED values rather than updating as you type. Its colour pair is now tested in BOTH directions (see below), so what a human adds there is narrower than first filed: whether the swatch opens the OS dialog at all, and whether picking a colour in it feels immediate. Scope it as a real review with someone who has not been building it.**\n`#frontend` `#ux`\n`TODO.md:L423`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 5640
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 481,
          "tags": [
            "#frontend",
            "#perf",
            "#critical"
          ],
          "text": "**Workshop is the next surface, elevated on production numbers** (Alexander, 2026-08-01). It was scheduled mid-Phase-3 behind Users. Measured on production, it is the worst surface in the product and the only one that misses the plan's throughput budget on every axis at once: **`/workshop/models` \u2014 144 requests, 20,520 kB, 6,181 kB transferred, 32 s**; **`/workshop/knowledge` \u2014 ~140 requests, 18,203 kB, 5,257 kB transferred, 19.07 s**. Against a budget of \u2264 ~2 MB transferred and first content under 2 s, and against the conversation baseline this migration exists to beat (172 req / 7.9 MB / ~19 s), a LIST OF MODELS is slower than a conversation. That makes it the strongest available evidence for the plan's second leg, which so far rests on the chat route alone. 5,567 lines across models/knowledge/prompts; the table predicts ~50\u201365%. **Read, not yet measured** \u2014 the last two times this was assumed the assumption was wrong (the 22 MB WASM and the markdown libs both turned out already lazy), so these are candidates and the first task is a before-measurement: the route awaits `getModels` (the provider probe) _before_ rendering `<Models />`, which only then fetches `getWorkshopModels` + `getGroups` + `getBranding`, so the waterfall is serial across a component boundary; `Models.svelte` statically imports `marked`, `sortablejs` and `file-saver`; `Knowledge.svelte` statically imports `fuse.js` and `dayjs` + `relativeTime`; and every row renders `model.meta.profile_image_url`, which in this lineage is often a base64 data URI and would inflate the payload rather than the request count."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 482,
          "tags": [
            "#frontend",
            "#perf",
            "#critical"
          ],
          "text": "**The list payload repeats the same user avatar once per agent \u2014 MEASURED on a production snapshot, and it is not the defect I first filed** (2026-08-01). Alexander: _\"Maybe use the backup for realistic tests\"_ \u2014 right, and it overturned the hypothesis below within one query. The agent-avatar theory does not survive contact with `tools/db_snapshots/webui.db.sqlite`: of its 324 `model` rows only **16** carry a data URI at all, and all agent `meta` totals **868 kB**. Not 20 MB, not close. **What the workshop list actually costs there** \u2014 and note the filter, because `Models.get_models()` returns only `base_model_id IS NOT NULL`, so most of those 324 rows are base models and never appear: **48 agents, 1,032 kB of row data, plus 475 kB of embedded owner avatars \u2248 1.5 MB.** **The 475 kB is the interesting part, because the page renders none of it.** `GET /api/v1/models/` returns `list[ModelUserResponse]`, which nests the owner's whole `UserResponse` in every row, and 21 of the instance's 32 users carry a base64 avatar \u2014 those users hold **120 kB** between them, amplified ~4\u00d7 by being repeated per agent they own. The row displays \"By {name}\" as text, so roughly a third of the response is downloaded, parsed and dropped. A server-rendered page does not reduce that cost, it **does not incur it**: the row context holds a name string. First place in this migration where the fragment path shows up as bytes rather than lines. **Also found**: `Models.get_models()` calls `Users.get_user_by_id()` inside its loop \u2014 one query per agent, so 49 queries for 48 rows. **What is still NOT explained is the gap to 20 MB**, and guessing at it twice is enough: boot the snapshot with `KEEP=1 make upgrade_gate` and measure the real page. 48 rows also means 48 tooltips and 48 `marked.parse()` calls, and the conversation baseline is already 13.4 MB decoded before this page adds anything."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 483,
          "tags": [
            "#frontend",
            "#perf",
            "#critical"
          ],
          "text": "~~**Agent avatars are uncompressed PNG data URIs inline in the list payload**~~ \u2014 **REFUTED 2026-08-01 by the snapshot above, and kept because being wrong here is the third time this exact way.** The reading was correct as far as it went: `ModelEditor.svelte:366` really does store avatars with `canvas.toDataURL()` \u2014 no arguments, so PNG \u2014 at a fixed 250\u00d7250 for a 44px slot, and `meta` really does ride whole in the list response. The arithmetic was correct too: one such avatar measures 172 kB of PNG, 230 kB as base64. **What was wrong was assuming the worst case is the common case.** Real instances have a few avatars, not one per agent. This now joins the 22 MB WASM and the markdown libs as inferences that read perfectly and measured false \u2014 and all three were caught the same way, by measuring before acting. Keep the format fix (`toDataURL('image/webp', 0.8)` and dimensions matched to the slot) as a small, cheap, separate item; it is right, it is just not the 20 MB. #frontend #perf `ModelEditor.svelte:366` stores the avatar as `canvas.toDataURL()` \u2014 **no arguments, so it defaults to PNG** \u2014 at a fixed 250\u00d7250, then writes it into `info.meta.profile_image_url`. `GET /api/v1/models/` returns `list[ModelUserResponse]`, which carries `meta` whole with no projection, so **every avatar rides in every list response**, and the list renders it at 44px. Measured cost of one such avatar, photo-like worst case: **172.1 kB of PNG \u2192 229.5 kB as base64 (+33%)**; 40 agents is 8.96 MB and 60 is 13.45 MB in a single response, against a page measured at 20,520 kB. A flat logo compresses far better, so the real figure depends on what people upload \u2014 **the decisive test is one `curl` against production: the byte size of `/api/v1/models/` and the agent count.** Three faults compounding: the format (a `toDataURL('image/webp', 0.8)` is roughly an order of magnitude smaller), the dimensions (250\u00d7250 stored for a 44px slot), and the storage shape (inline, so it can never be cached separately, lazy-loaded, or served conditionally). **The third one follows the surface**: server-rendering this page moves the base64 out of JSON and into the HTML rather than removing it, so a migration that ports it faithfully would ship the same 20 MB with better line counts. Fix it AS the migration \u2014 \"prune, don't port\". **Checked, and it is worse than a workshop problem: the avatars ride in TWO endpoints and this page fetches both.** `utils/models.py:175` sets `model[\"info\"] = custom_model.model_dump()`, so `/api/models` carries the whole custom model including the base64 \u2014 and that is the endpoint the app-wide boot ladder calls on **every page**, not just this one. `/api/v1/models/` carries it a second time for the workshop list. So the conversation baseline (172 req / 7.9 MB) is probably paying this too, and `/workshop/models` pays it **twice per load**: once in the route's `onMount` and once in `Models.svelte`. That reframes the fix as an app-wide payload change with the workshop page as its worst case, and it is worth its own before/after independent of the migration."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 484,
          "tags": [
            "#frontend",
            "#perf"
          ],
          "text": "**Avatars: right-size them at the encoder, and serve them from a cacheable URL \u2014 30\u00d7 measured, no new dependency** (Alexander, 2026-08-01: _\"for avatars we should think about making it so that they are cached at various sizes. We can really go small and fast this way.\"_ Right about the direction; the numbers say **one** right size beats a multi-size cache here). Measured on three real avatars pulled from the production snapshot, all stored 250\u00d7250: an agent avatar is **106.8 / 97.7 / 95.0 kB** because it is PNG; the same image as JPEG is **21 kB**, at 128px **5.9\u20137.2 kB**, at 88px **3.6\u20134.5 kB**. So format alone is **5\u00d7** and format-plus-size is **30\u00d7**. **The root cause is three copies of one function.** `ModelEditor.svelte:366`, `Settings/Account.svelte:134` and `ArenaModelModal.svelte:202` are the same canvas-resize block pasted three times at the same 250\u00d7250 \u2014 and only the agent one calls `toDataURL()` with **no format argument**, so it defaults to PNG while the other two pass `'image/jpeg'`. That is the written-twice drift this migration exists to delete, showing up as a 5\u00d7 payload difference between two avatars a user cannot tell apart. Fix at the encoder: one shared helper, WebP with a JPEG fallback. **DECIDED 2026-08-01 (Alexander): migration AND serve-time cache, not the encoder-only fix I first recommended.** That call is right and my framing was wrong in a way worth writing down \u2014 I argued it as either/or on payload size, but **the migration's real job is not shrinking, it is getting the blob out of the database row.** Encoder-only leaves the image inside `meta`, where it rides every response that returns `meta`, cannot be cached separately, and bloats every row and backup; and picking one size at upload is destructive, so a later size needs the original that no longer exists. The two halves compose: the migration moves originals to content-addressed storage on the data volume and leaves a reference in the row; the cache derives sizes beside them on first request; a future size costs nothing. **I also overstated the security cost** \u2014 the server already ingests and stores these images through an authenticated POST, so adding a decode raises the surface rather than opening it. Standard hardening covers it: a format allowlist, a byte ceiling, and `Image.MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`. Pillow is still a genuine new dependency, verified absent from `requirements.txt` and from the built image. **The trap that must land WITH the migration, not after: exports break.** `Models.svelte:167` and `admin/Settings/Models.svelte:68` both `JSON.stringify` the model objects verbatim, so an export is self-contained today \u2014 a data URI travels with it and imports anywhere. Replace that with a local path and every export becomes non-portable, silently, and only when someone imports it into another instance. The export paths have to re-inline from storage. **Design constraints, each earned:** sizes come from a CLOSED set (44/128/256), never a free query parameter, or `?s=1..2000` is render amplification that fills the volume; the cache lives on the volume rather than in memory, because multi-worker is on the roadmap and an in-process cache would duplicate per worker; the migration is non-destructive (write the file, verify, then clear the row), idempotent, and fails closed when the volume is not writable \u2014 `data_not_writable` is already a diagnostics issue_type. `make upgrade_gate` boots a real snapshot and is the proof."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 485,
          "tags": [
            "#frontend",
            "#brand"
          ],
          "text": "**Rename `/workshop/models` \u2192 `/workshop/agents`** (Alexander, 2026-08-01: _\"should really be agents\"_). The UI **already says Agents** \u2014 `Models.svelte` renders `$i18n.t('Agents')` as the heading, the page title, \"Import Agents\" and \"Export Agents\"; only the URL, the directory and the component names still say models, so this is finishing a rename someone started rather than beginning one. What the surface holds is not models \u2014 it is a model plus a system prompt plus knowledge plus tools, which is an agent preset. Consistent with the locked 2026-07-11 decision for **one tiered \"Agents\" surface**, where extended (Sprig-delivered) agents appear alongside these with a tier badge; that decision's \"no renames\" clause was about not splitting the tiers into separate surfaces, not about keeping this name. Do it AS the migration rather than before it \u2014 the route is being rebuilt anyway, and renaming twice costs two redirect layers. Needs: the route, the nav label, the i18n keys, and a redirect from the old path for anyone holding a bookmark."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 486,
          "tags": [
            "#biomes",
            "#ratchet"
          ],
          "text": "Count and pin the core biomes as a downward-only ratchet \u2014 chat core, rich-text editor, code editor, flow canvas, channels; the count only decreases"
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 487,
          "tags": [
            "#frontend"
          ],
          "text": "**Mid-term: rebuild the chat core as an htmx flow, not a ported island** (Alexander, 2026-07-27). The plan currently treats the chat core as the worst-value surface \u2014 17,591 lines for a 15\u201330% cut \u2014 on the assumption it gets ported to islands, where app-shaped code changes dialect rather than disappearing. An htmx-first rebuild is a different bet: the server owns the conversation and sends fragments, and only the token stream stays vanilla (Phase S already proved that half in ~260 lines, abort semantics included). Two Phase 0 findings point the same way \u2014 the island path did NOT shrink a form surface while the fragment path is the one the estimates assumed, and the runtime probe showed a biome is a real boundary to work across, not a free wrapper. Prerequisites before this is more than a direction: the Phase 1 cookie bridge (fragments need the token off localStorage), the non-proxying browser driver Phase S made a condition, and a fragment surface actually measured. Do NOT re-plan the phase order on this yet \u2014 it is a hypothesis with two supporting datapoints, and the thing that would settle it is one migrated fragment surface."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 488,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "Reimplement the flow canvas (`@xyflow/svelte`) framework-free"
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 489,
          "tags": [
            "#brand"
          ],
          "text": "Slogan precision: \"org-owned\" not \"user-owned\"; keep yjs local-first for the editor"
        }
      ],
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "6e33ab88a22eb8bf",
      "line_num": 480,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Phases 3\u20134 \u2014 medium surfaces, shell flip, then biomes: workshop FIRST (see below), then users, evals, settings tabs, chat list \u2192 prune biomes (Functions, then chat core last, gated on the spike memo).**\n- [ ] **Workshop is the next surface, elevated on production numbers** (Alexander, 2026-08-01). It was scheduled mid-Phase-3 behind Users. Measured on production, it is the worst surface in the product and the only one that misses the plan's throughput budget on every axis at once: **`/workshop/models` \u2014 144 requests, 20,520 kB, 6,181 kB transferred, 32 s**; **`/workshop/knowledge` \u2014 ~140 requests, 18,203 kB, 5,257 kB transferred, 19.07 s**. Against a budget of \u2264 ~2 MB transferred and first content under 2 s, and against the conversation baseline this migration exists to beat (172 req / 7.9 MB / ~19 s), a LIST OF MODELS is slower than a conversation. That makes it the strongest available evidence for the plan's second leg, which so far rests on the chat route alone. 5,567 lines across models/knowledge/prompts; the table predicts ~50\u201365%. **Read, not yet measured** \u2014 the last two times this was assumed the assumption was wrong (the 22 MB WASM and the markdown libs both turned out already lazy), so these are candidates and the first task is a before-measurement: the route awaits `getModels` (the provider probe) _before_ rendering `<Models />`, which only then fetches `getWorkshopModels` + `getGroups` + `getBranding`, so the waterfall is serial across a component boundary; `Models.svelte` statically imports `marked`, `sortablejs` and `file-saver`; `Knowledge.svelte` statically imports `fuse.js` and `dayjs` + `relativeTime`; and every row renders `model.meta.profile_image_url`, which in this lineage is often a base64 data URI and would inflate the payload rather than the request count. #frontend #perf #critical\n- [ ] **The list payload repeats the same user avatar once per agent \u2014 MEASURED on a production snapshot, and it is not the defect I first filed** (2026-08-01). Alexander: _\"Maybe use the backup for realistic tests\"_ \u2014 right, and it overturned the hypothesis below within one query. The agent-avatar theory does not survive contact with `tools/db_snapshots/webui.db.sqlite`: of its 324 `model` rows only **16** carry a data URI at all, and all agent `meta` totals **868 kB**. Not 20 MB, not close. **What the workshop list actually costs there** \u2014 and note the filter, because `Models.get_models()` returns only `base_model_id IS NOT NULL`, so most of those 324 rows are base models and never appear: **48 agents, 1,032 kB of row data, plus 475 kB of embedded owner avatars \u2248 1.5 MB.** **The 475 kB is the interesting part, because the page renders none of it.** `GET /api/v1/models/` returns `list[ModelUserResponse]`, which nests the owner's whole `UserResponse` in every row, and 21 of the instance's 32 users carry a base64 avatar \u2014 those users hold **120 kB** between them, amplified ~4\u00d7 by being repeated per agent they own. The row displays \"By {name}\" as text, so roughly a third of the response is downloaded, parsed and dropped. A server-rendered page does not reduce that cost, it **does not incur it**: the row context holds a name string. First place in this migration where the fragment path shows up as bytes rather than lines. **Also found**: `Models.get_models()` calls `Users.get_user_by_id()` inside its loop \u2014 one query per agent, so 49 queries for 48 rows. **What is still NOT explained is the gap to 20 MB**, and guessing at it twice is enough: boot the snapshot with `KEEP=1 make upgrade_gate` and measure the real page. 48 rows also means 48 tooltips and 48 `marked.parse()` calls, and the conversation baseline is already 13.4 MB decoded before this page adds anything. #frontend #perf #critical\n- [ ] ~~**Agent avatars are uncompressed PNG data URIs inline in the list payload**~~ \u2014 **REFUTED 2026-08-01 by the snapshot above, and kept because being wrong here is the third time this exact way.** The reading was correct as far as it went: `ModelEditor.svelte:366` really does store avatars with `canvas.toDataURL()` \u2014 no arguments, so PNG \u2014 at a fixed 250\u00d7250 for a 44px slot, and `meta` really does ride whole in the list response. The arithmetic was correct too: one such avatar measures 172 kB of PNG, 230 kB as base64. **What was wrong was assuming the worst case is the common case.** Real instances have a few avatars, not one per agent. This now joins the 22 MB WASM and the markdown libs as inferences that read perfectly and measured false \u2014 and all three were caught the same way, by measuring before acting. Keep the format fix (`toDataURL('image/webp', 0.8)` and dimensions matched to the slot) as a small, cheap, separate item; it is right, it is just not the 20 MB. #frontend #perf `ModelEditor.svelte:366` stores the avatar as `canvas.toDataURL()` \u2014 **no arguments, so it defaults to PNG** \u2014 at a fixed 250\u00d7250, then writes it into `info.meta.profile_image_url`. `GET /api/v1/models/` returns `list[ModelUserResponse]`, which carries `meta` whole with no projection, so **every avatar rides in every list response**, and the list renders it at 44px. Measured cost of one such avatar, photo-like worst case: **172.1 kB of PNG \u2192 229.5 kB as base64 (+33%)**; 40 agents is 8.96 MB and 60 is 13.45 MB in a single response, against a page measured at 20,520 kB. A flat logo compresses far better, so the real figure depends on what people upload \u2014 **the decisive test is one `curl` against production: the byte size of `/api/v1/models/` and the agent count.** Three faults compounding: the format (a `toDataURL('image/webp', 0.8)` is roughly an order of magnitude smaller), the dimensions (250\u00d7250 stored for a 44px slot), and the storage shape (inline, so it can never be cached separately, lazy-loaded, or served conditionally). **The third one follows the surface**: server-rendering this page moves the base64 out of JSON and into the HTML rather than removing it, so a migration that ports it faithfully would ship the same 20 MB with better line counts. Fix it AS the migration \u2014 \"prune, don't port\". **Checked, and it is worse than a workshop problem: the avatars ride in TWO endpoints and this page fetches both.** `utils/models.py:175` sets `model[\"info\"] = custom_model.model_dump()`, so `/api/models` carries the whole custom model including the base64 \u2014 and that is the endpoint the app-wide boot ladder calls on **every page**, not just this one. `/api/v1/models/` carries it a second time for the workshop list. So the conversation baseline (172 req / 7.9 MB) is probably paying this too, and `/workshop/models` pays it **twice per load**: once in the route's `onMount` and once in `Models.svelte`. That reframes the fix as an app-wide payload change with the workshop page as its worst case, and it is worth its own before/after independent of the migration. #frontend #perf #critical\n- [ ] **Avatars: right-size them at the encoder, and serve them from a cacheable URL \u2014 30\u00d7 measured, no new dependency** (Alexander, 2026-08-01: _\"for avatars we should think about making it so that they are cached at various sizes. We can really go small and fast this way.\"_ Right about the direction; the numbers say **one** right size beats a multi-size cache here). Measured on three real avatars pulled from the production snapshot, all stored 250\u00d7250: an agent avatar is **106.8 / 97.7 / 95.0 kB** because it is PNG; the same image as JPEG is **21 kB**, at 128px **5.9\u20137.2 kB**, at 88px **3.6\u20134.5 kB**. So format alone is **5\u00d7** and format-plus-size is **30\u00d7**. **The root cause is three copies of one function.** `ModelEditor.svelte:366`, `Settings/Account.svelte:134` and `ArenaModelModal.svelte:202` are the same canvas-resize block pasted three times at the same 250\u00d7250 \u2014 and only the agent one calls `toDataURL()` with **no format argument**, so it defaults to PNG while the other two pass `'image/jpeg'`. That is the written-twice drift this migration exists to delete, showing up as a 5\u00d7 payload difference between two avatars a user cannot tell apart. Fix at the encoder: one shared helper, WebP with a JPEG fallback. **DECIDED 2026-08-01 (Alexander): migration AND serve-time cache, not the encoder-only fix I first recommended.** That call is right and my framing was wrong in a way worth writing down \u2014 I argued it as either/or on payload size, but **the migration's real job is not shrinking, it is getting the blob out of the database row.** Encoder-only leaves the image inside `meta`, where it rides every response that returns `meta`, cannot be cached separately, and bloats every row and backup; and picking one size at upload is destructive, so a later size needs the original that no longer exists. The two halves compose: the migration moves originals to content-addressed storage on the data volume and leaves a reference in the row; the cache derives sizes beside them on first request; a future size costs nothing. **I also overstated the security cost** \u2014 the server already ingests and stores these images through an authenticated POST, so adding a decode raises the surface rather than opening it. Standard hardening covers it: a format allowlist, a byte ceiling, and `Image.MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS`. Pillow is still a genuine new dependency, verified absent from `requirements.txt` and from the built image. **The trap that must land WITH the migration, not after: exports break.** `Models.svelte:167` and `admin/Settings/Models.svelte:68` both `JSON.stringify` the model objects verbatim, so an export is self-contained today \u2014 a data URI travels with it and imports anywhere. Replace that with a local path and every export becomes non-portable, silently, and only when someone imports it into another instance. The export paths have to re-inline from storage. **Design constraints, each earned:** sizes come from a CLOSED set (44/128/256), never a free query parameter, or `?s=1..2000` is render amplification that fills the volume; the cache lives on the volume rather than in memory, because multi-worker is on the roadmap and an in-process cache would duplicate per worker; the migration is non-destructive (write the file, verify, then clear the row), idempotent, and fails closed when the volume is not writable \u2014 `data_not_writable` is already a diagnostics issue_type. `make upgrade_gate` boots a real snapshot and is the proof. #frontend #perf\n- [ ] **Rename `/workshop/models` \u2192 `/workshop/agents`** (Alexander, 2026-08-01: _\"should really be agents\"_). The UI **already says Agents** \u2014 `Models.svelte` renders `$i18n.t('Agents')` as the heading, the page title, \"Import Agents\" and \"Export Agents\"; only the URL, the directory and the component names still say models, so this is finishing a rename someone started rather than beginning one. What the surface holds is not models \u2014 it is a model plus a system prompt plus knowledge plus tools, which is an agent preset. Consistent with the locked 2026-07-11 decision for **one tiered \"Agents\" surface**, where extended (Sprig-delivered) agents appear alongside these with a tier badge; that decision's \"no renames\" clause was about not splitting the tiers into separate surfaces, not about keeping this name. Do it AS the migration rather than before it \u2014 the route is being rebuilt anyway, and renaming twice costs two redirect layers. Needs: the route, the nav label, the i18n keys, and a redirect from the old path for anyone holding a bookmark. #frontend #brand\n- [ ] Count and pin the core biomes as a downward-only ratchet \u2014 chat core, rich-text editor, code editor, flow canvas, channels; the count only decreases #biomes #ratchet\n- [ ] **Mid-term: rebuild the chat core as an htmx flow, not a ported island** (Alexander, 2026-07-27). The plan currently treats the chat core as the worst-value surface \u2014 17,591 lines for a 15\u201330% cut \u2014 on the assumption it gets ported to islands, where app-shaped code changes dialect rather than disappearing. An htmx-first rebuild is a different bet: the server owns the conversation and sends fragments, and only the token stream stays vanilla (Phase S already proved that half in ~260 lines, abort semantics included). Two Phase 0 findings point the same way \u2014 the island path did NOT shrink a form surface while the fragment path is the one the estimates assumed, and the runtime probe showed a biome is a real boundary to work across, not a free wrapper. Prerequisites before this is more than a direction: the Phase 1 cookie bridge (fragments need the token off localStorage), the non-proxying browser driver Phase S made a condition, and a fragment surface actually measured. Do NOT re-plan the phase order on this yet \u2014 it is a hypothesis with two supporting datapoints, and the thing that would settle it is one migrated fragment surface. #frontend\n- [ ] Reimplement the flow canvas (`@xyflow/svelte`) framework-free\n- [ ] Slogan precision: \"org-owned\" not \"user-owned\"; keep yjs local-first for the editor #brand\n\n`TODO.md:L480`",
