      "x": 1400,
      "y": 0
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 409,
          "tags": [
            "#frontend"
          ],
          "text": "**Re-measured with a server-rendered FRAGMENT \u2014 157 lines, a 32% cut, and the estimate is close after all** (2026-07-27; spec 10/10 against the fragment, `make e2e` 42 tests/39 passing/3 pending by design, `ui_sprig_gate` 22/22, `sprig_smoke` 66/66). Same panel, third implementation, non-comment lines: **Svelte 231** (215 + ~16 wrapper) \u00b7 **island 233** (189 + 44) \u00b7 **fragment 157** (109 render + 48 routes, and **zero** client JS beyond one shared vendored htmx), against a plan predicting 238 \u2192 ~150. **The first fragment draft was 208, and that number was wrong about the method rather than the approach.** It came down by three things, all found by asking whether it could be cleaner: two near-identical Graft/Prune branches collapsed into one described button; the capability and the action verb moved out of the request and into the path, so the two lifecycle endpoints became one with a `Literal[\"graft\",\"prune\"]` FastAPI refuses off-list (the guard-rail asserts the 422); and a hardcoded table of prune-reset sentences moved into the prune response as `messages`, which is the real fix, since the Svelte panel and the island each carried their own copy. **A first draft of a migrated surface is not its measurement.** Roughly half the remaining 109 lines are hand-built HTML f-strings, so the floor is ~157 by hand and plausibly ~110 templated. The island is deleted; three implementations of one panel is the duplication this migration exists to remove. htmx 2.0.4 vendored with a provenance chain recorded \u2014 note for the next person: npm's `dist.integrity` is a TARBALL digest and will never match a file's own hash."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 410,
          "tags": [
            "#frontend"
          ],
          "text": "**Add a template engine and re-measure a third time** \u2014 ~150 of the fragment's 208 lines are hand-built HTML strings. jinja2 is not in the image (checked), so it is a real dependency decision, but it now has a number attached rather than a preference: it is the difference between a 10% cut and the plan's 40%. Do it when the second and third fragment surfaces land, so the choice is made against three call sites and not one."
        }
      ],
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "539db714a8dd8608",
      "line_num": 408,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#frontend",
        "#critical"
      ],
      "text": "**Phase 0 \u2014 seam + Sprigs pilot SHIPPED, and it did NOT shrink the code (2026-07-27; `make e2e` 31 tests/30 passing/1 pending by design; committed). The seam: `sage_is_ai/pages/` included in `main.py` at `/pages`, with `/pages/_assets` on `CachedStaticFiles`, both registered BEFORE the SPA catch-all so the compiled bundle never sees them. `/themes/active.css` already relied on that ordering; naming it makes it a seam rather than a coincidence. Assets get a week-long cache plus `?v=VERSION` busting, because there is no build step to content-hash them \u2014 which is the point. The pilot: `/pages/admin/sprigs` talks straight to `/api/v1/retrieval/sprigs/{catalog,graft,prune}` instead of through the generated wrapper layer. The proof: `sprigs-panel.cy.ts` green against both \u2014 7/7 legacy, 8/8 no-build \u2014 and the extra test is the one that stops it passing vacuously, asserting no `_app/immutable` script is on the page. The uncomfortable measurement: predicted 238 \u2192 ~150; measured Svelte 215 + ~16 wrapper against island 189 + 44 route = 233, so the pilot came out slightly BIGGER per surface. Why that is not a refutation: this panel is a list with buttons that POST and re-fetch, so by the plan's own island test it is fragment-shaped \u2014 and a fragment needs the operator's token, which lived in localStorage until the Phase 1 cookie bridge. The ISLAND path does not shrink a form surface; the FRAGMENT path is the one the estimate assumed, and it was structurally unavailable this phase. Treat the table's other rows as unmeasured until a fragment surface lands. Second limit: the island fetches with the localStorage token, so there is no first-paint win here.**\n- [x] **Re-measured with a server-rendered FRAGMENT \u2014 157 lines, a 32% cut, and the estimate is close after all** (2026-07-27; spec 10/10 against the fragment, `make e2e` 42 tests/39 passing/3 pending by design, `ui_sprig_gate` 22/22, `sprig_smoke` 66/66). Same panel, third implementation, non-comment lines: **Svelte 231** (215 + ~16 wrapper) \u00b7 **island 233** (189 + 44) \u00b7 **fragment 157** (109 render + 48 routes, and **zero** client JS beyond one shared vendored htmx), against a plan predicting 238 \u2192 ~150. **The first fragment draft was 208, and that number was wrong about the method rather than the approach.** It came down by three things, all found by asking whether it could be cleaner: two near-identical Graft/Prune branches collapsed into one described button; the capability and the action verb moved out of the request and into the path, so the two lifecycle endpoints became one with a `Literal[\"graft\",\"prune\"]` FastAPI refuses off-list (the guard-rail asserts the 422); and a hardcoded table of prune-reset sentences moved into the prune response as `messages`, which is the real fix, since the Svelte panel and the island each carried their own copy. **A first draft of a migrated surface is not its measurement.** Roughly half the remaining 109 lines are hand-built HTML f-strings, so the floor is ~157 by hand and plausibly ~110 templated. The island is deleted; three implementations of one panel is the duplication this migration exists to remove. htmx 2.0.4 vendored with a provenance chain recorded \u2014 note for the next person: npm's `dist.integrity` is a TARBALL digest and will never match a file's own hash. #frontend\n- [ ] **Add a template engine and re-measure a third time** \u2014 ~150 of the fragment's 208 lines are hand-built HTML strings. jinja2 is not in the image (checked), so it is a real dependency decision, but it now has a number attached rather than a preference: it is the difference between a 10% cut and the plan's 40%. Do it when the second and third fragment surfaces land, so the choice is made against three call sites and not one. #frontend\n\n`#frontend` `#critical`\n`TODO.md:L408`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 60
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 413,
          "tags": [
            "#sprigs",
            "#ui"
          ],
          "text": "Write \"no framework sprigs\" into the ui-Sprig spec repo \u2014 fragments + host runtime only; carry the THREE mechanisms above (grant, inline-and-same-origin, size ceiling) and the honest note that no static check decides \"is this a framework\""
        }
      ],
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "ec4905765d8ab40c",
      "line_num": 412,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#sprigs",
        "#ui",
        "#critical"
      ],
      "text": "**Phase 1 \u2014 ui-Sprig contract + cookie bridge SHIPPED (2026-07-27; `make e2e` 40 tests/39 passing/1 pending by design, `make sprig_smoke` 66/66, new `make ui_sprig_gate` 22/22; committed). The cookie bridge came in far under estimate. The plan treats it as a prerequisite subsystem, but `utils/auth.get_current_user` ALREADY falls back to the `token` cookie and every sign-in path already sets it httponly, so a render route had identity for free. What was actually missing is that a PAGE must fail differently from an API: `pages/auth.py` is ~30 lines turning a refusal into a 307 to `/auth?next=<path>` \u2014 path only, since a redirect target echoed from a request is an open redirect \u2014 with a signed-in non-admin getting 403 instead, because bouncing them to sign-in would loop. A near-miss worth recording: the first draft decoded the JWT itself and within twenty lines had drifted twice \u2014 it added a session check the API path does not make, and MISSED the try.sage session-reset cutoff it does, so a facilitator pressing \"Reset now\" would have invalidated every API session while leaving these pages signed in. The audit counted authorization restated ~142 times in the frontend; a second copy in the BACKEND is the same disease with a shorter incubation. `pages-cookie-auth.cy.ts` 4/4, including the non-admin branch and that the cookie is httpOnly. The ui-Sprig contract: `sprigs/ui_dispatch.py` mirroring `theme` exactly, `SPRIG_ACTIVE_UI` / `SPRIG_UI_SCRIPTING_GRANT` pointers, `/ui/active.html`, a server-rendered slot, `POST /sprigs/ui/scripting`, and a first-party example published to the local registry. Lifecycle proven end to end against the real artifact (`ui-sprig-contract.cy.ts` 5/5). The grant is held by NAME, not as a boolean \u2014 a decision about a fragment an admin read cannot be inherited by whatever is grafted next \u2014 and both readers revalidate, so revoking works without a regraft. \"No framework sprigs\" is enforced three ways, and the memo is honest that no regex decides it: scripting off unless granted; inline-only and same-origin when granted; and a 16 kB script ceiling, sized from the runtime probe's ~18 kB minimum for an independently-built biome. `make ui_sprig_gate` proves 22 REFUSALS against the real validator, asserting the REASON rather than the outcome \u2014 and that script passed silently on its first run because `docker run` without `-i` gave python empty stdin, so it now carries a sentinel. Deliberately NOT done: an `app.html` slot for the SPA, since the slot lives in the surface that survives the migration.**\n- [ ] Write \"no framework sprigs\" into the ui-Sprig spec repo \u2014 fragments + host runtime only; carry the THREE mechanisms above (grant, inline-and-same-origin, size ceiling) and the honest note that no static check decides \"is this a framework\" #sprigs #ui\n\n`#sprigs` `#ui` `#critical`\n`TODO.md:L412`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 150
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "589277133dcdfcb0",
      "line_num": 415,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#frontend",
        "#critical"
      ],
      "text": "**Migration test discipline made mechanical \u2014 surface registry, dual-target runner, parity gate (Alexander, 2026-07-28: specs \"should work against both the original Svelte and the new build by just being pointed at them\"; `gauntlet_full` exit 0, both targets green \u2014 legacy 55 tests/52 passing/3 pending, nobuild 55/55). The sharper half of the criticism is that writing the spec first is NOT enough when the person writing it also chooses what it covers: the diagnostics guard-rail was written against Svelte, passed against both, and the no-build page was still missing most of the old one \u2014 plus a \"Show me how to fix this\" button that rendered and did nothing. Every assertion passed because I had written assertions for the parts I had built. Three devices. (1) `app/cypress/support/surfaces.ts` \u2014 one registry mapping a surface NAME to its two paths, replacing the per-spec env vars each spec invented. Registering a surface is the FIRST step of migrating it, because that is when \"what does the old one actually do?\" still has to be answered. (2) `make e2e_both` runs the whole suite once per target, so \"green against both\" is what the gate means rather than what somebody remembered to check. (3) `surface-parity.cy.ts` \u2014 the check that does not depend on my judgement. It asserts nothing about behaviour: it collects the `data-cy` hooks each implementation RENDERS and fails if the no-build page is missing any. It cannot be satisfied by writing a narrower spec, because it never reads the spec. It went RED on first run and named the work: `diag-command-library, diag-ghost-endpoints, diag-reprobe, diag-technical` \u2014 its first failure said only \"expected [ Array(4) ] to deeply equal []\", so it asserts on a joined string now and the failure carries the work list. All four were then built, taking diagnostics from ~40% of the old surface to parity. Honest limit written into the spec: identical hooks are a coverage FLOOR, not proof of equivalence \u2014 a button that renders and does nothing has the same hook as one that works.**\n`#frontend` `#critical`\n`TODO.md:L415`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 240
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 404,
          "tags": [
            "#frontend",
            "#biomes",
            "#critical"
          ],
          "text": "**Spike-prove the shared-runtime assumption \u2014 HOLDS** (2026-07-27, `tools/spikes/biomes/`, memo at `MEMO.md`, `run.sh` re-runs it). **16/16 checks across three build shapes, four of them against a shape that MUST fail** \u2014 a suite that has never said no is not evidence. **Shape A** (one Vite build, three biomes \u2014 the Phase 3 flip): one runtime, one shared-store instance, a store written in biome A observed in biome B, all mounted from plain HTML with no SvelteKit. Identity, not resemblance \u2014 each biome hands the page its own reference to Svelte's scheduler and the page compares them. **Shape B** (three independent builds, the CONTROL): three runtimes, no sharing, and the probe detects all of it. **Shape C** (a biome built LATER, borrowing the host runtime by URL \u2014 the ui-Sprig case): 1.6 kB, shares both. **Three costs the plan had not priced.** (1) \"Externalise the runtime\" is not one specifier \u2014 marking `svelte/internal` external and leaving `svelte` alone builds clean and throws `Function called outside component initialization` at mount; in a marketplace that fails in someone else's deployment after graft. (2) Publishing the runtime defeats tree-shaking: 27.3 kB \u2192 95.4 kB unminified. Phase 3 does not pay it; the Sprig marketplace does, after which each late biome costs 1.6 kB. (3) Vite's default `preserveEntrySignatures: false` ships mangled export names, unusable as an interface. All three are one shape \u2014 the host must publish a deliberate interface, and every default is against it. Also measured: a biome is a CSS boundary, and `shadow: 'none'` removes the boundary and the encapsulation together. The `$app/*` surface is 64 of 317 components and nine symbols, `goto`+`page` being 80 of 81 sites, shimmed in ~50 lines. **What it does NOT say**: no biome was put on a page beside the legacy SvelteKit bundle, and these biomes are toys \u2014 the chat core's 710 handlers and 859 bindings are untested. Phase 4 unblocked on runtime grounds."
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 405,
          "tags": [
            "#sprigs",
            "#ui"
          ],
          "text": "Write costs (1) and (2) above into the ui-Sprig spec \u2014 they are the MECHANISM behind \"no framework sprigs\", not a matter of taste"
        },
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 406,
          "tags": [
            "#perf",
            "#bonsai"
          ],
          "text": "**Version `/themes/active.css` once the shell is server-rendered** (deferred here from the 2026-07-26 caching pass. **That premise is now false and the item is NOT blocked**: the panels moved to Jinja2 on 2026-08-01 but `shell.py` deliberately did not \u2014 it interpolates three escaped values against four raw ones, so a template would need four `| safe`, which is the hatch autoescape exists to avoid. Nothing here needs templating anyway: `shell.asset_url()` already version-stamps by release, and this wants the same treatment plus an ETag). MEASURED on the shipped image with no theme grafted \u2014 the common case: `HTTP 200`, `cache-control: no-cache`, **no ETag, no Last-Modified**, and a conditional request with `If-None-Match` still returns **200, not 304**. So every full page load spends a render-blocking round-trip on 23 bytes of `/* no theme grafted */`, via the static `<link>` at `app.html:21`. Do NOT invalidate this on image/container updates: `SPRIG_ACTIVE_THEME` is flipped by a graft and cleared by a prune at RUNTIME (`routers/sprigs.py:151,305`) with no restart, so deploy-keyed invalidation would leave an operator's freshly grafted theme invisible until restart \u2014 the exact failure the Sprig durability work exists to prevent. Right design: inject `?v=<active theme name + css mtime>` into the shell's `<link>` at serve time, then serve the CSS `immutable, max-age=31536000`. A graft changes the token, so the URL changes, so pickup is instant AND there is zero revalidation in between (same trick SvelteKit uses for `_app/immutable`). Interim options considered and rejected for now: a short TTL (a grafted theme could appear one load late) and an ETag-only fix (saves a 23-byte body, not the round-trip)."
        }
      ],
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "0a850afaeb6a5948",
      "line_num": 403,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#frontend"
      ],
      "text": "**Phase S \u2014 streaming spike: GO, with two conditions (2026-07-27, `tools/spikes/streaming/`, memo at `MEMO.md`). All four done-criteria measured, not asserted: HTTP/2 (`http_version 2` on shell, island and vendored htmx, via Caddy `flush_interval -1` \u2014 without that line the proxy buffers and the spike looks fine while proving nothing); incremental delivery (frame 1 at 0.000 s \u2192 frame 150 at 3.337 s); stop really stops \u2014 client `{outcome:\"stopped\", tokens:219/453}` AND server `stream_aborted sent=21 total=164`, which is the half that matters, since a stop button that only hides output is theatre; autoscroll holds. 8/8 under Playwright, TTFT 5 ms, ~260 lines of stdlib server + framework-free island. Two findings that change the plan. (1) Cypress cannot see streaming \u2014 it MITM-proxies the AUT and buffers the body, so the page observes nothing until the stream ends. Not fixable with better assertions; any streaming guard-rail needs a non-proxying driver. (2) Autoscroll took four attempts, and attempt 3 passed every automated check while being broken on real hardware \u2014 inferring intent from gesture deltas went 13/13 green under Playwright and released the pin on PAGE LOAD, from trackpad jitter. Shipped: judge by POSITION on every scroll event, plus a rule refusing to yank a reader who has moved the box. No gesture listeners at all, so momentum, scrollbar drag and keyboard paging come free. GO is conditional: a non-proxying driver lands before any streaming surface is cut \u2014 necessary but NOT sufficient, since attempt 3 was green under exactly such a driver \u2014 so every streamed surface needs a human pass before it is called done. Phase 4 stays gated on the shared-runtime probe; this spike tested vanilla streaming, which needs no runtime at all.**\n- [x] **Spike-prove the shared-runtime assumption \u2014 HOLDS** (2026-07-27, `tools/spikes/biomes/`, memo at `MEMO.md`, `run.sh` re-runs it). **16/16 checks across three build shapes, four of them against a shape that MUST fail** \u2014 a suite that has never said no is not evidence. **Shape A** (one Vite build, three biomes \u2014 the Phase 3 flip): one runtime, one shared-store instance, a store written in biome A observed in biome B, all mounted from plain HTML with no SvelteKit. Identity, not resemblance \u2014 each biome hands the page its own reference to Svelte's scheduler and the page compares them. **Shape B** (three independent builds, the CONTROL): three runtimes, no sharing, and the probe detects all of it. **Shape C** (a biome built LATER, borrowing the host runtime by URL \u2014 the ui-Sprig case): 1.6 kB, shares both. **Three costs the plan had not priced.** (1) \"Externalise the runtime\" is not one specifier \u2014 marking `svelte/internal` external and leaving `svelte` alone builds clean and throws `Function called outside component initialization` at mount; in a marketplace that fails in someone else's deployment after graft. (2) Publishing the runtime defeats tree-shaking: 27.3 kB \u2192 95.4 kB unminified. Phase 3 does not pay it; the Sprig marketplace does, after which each late biome costs 1.6 kB. (3) Vite's default `preserveEntrySignatures: false` ships mangled export names, unusable as an interface. All three are one shape \u2014 the host must publish a deliberate interface, and every default is against it. Also measured: a biome is a CSS boundary, and `shadow: 'none'` removes the boundary and the encapsulation together. The `$app/*` surface is 64 of 317 components and nine symbols, `goto`+`page` being 80 of 81 sites, shimmed in ~50 lines. **What it does NOT say**: no biome was put on a page beside the legacy SvelteKit bundle, and these biomes are toys \u2014 the chat core's 710 handlers and 859 bindings are untested. Phase 4 unblocked on runtime grounds. #frontend #biomes #critical\n- [ ] Write costs (1) and (2) above into the ui-Sprig spec \u2014 they are the MECHANISM behind \"no framework sprigs\", not a matter of taste #sprigs #ui\n- [ ] **Version `/themes/active.css` once the shell is server-rendered** (deferred here from the 2026-07-26 caching pass. **That premise is now false and the item is NOT blocked**: the panels moved to Jinja2 on 2026-08-01 but `shell.py` deliberately did not \u2014 it interpolates three escaped values against four raw ones, so a template would need four `| safe`, which is the hatch autoescape exists to avoid. Nothing here needs templating anyway: `shell.asset_url()` already version-stamps by release, and this wants the same treatment plus an ETag). MEASURED on the shipped image with no theme grafted \u2014 the common case: `HTTP 200`, `cache-control: no-cache`, **no ETag, no Last-Modified**, and a conditional request with `If-None-Match` still returns **200, not 304**. So every full page load spends a render-blocking round-trip on 23 bytes of `/* no theme grafted */`, via the static `<link>` at `app.html:21`. Do NOT invalidate this on image/container updates: `SPRIG_ACTIVE_THEME` is flipped by a graft and cleared by a prune at RUNTIME (`routers/sprigs.py:151,305`) with no restart, so deploy-keyed invalidation would leave an operator's freshly grafted theme invisible until restart \u2014 the exact failure the Sprig durability work exists to prevent. Right design: inject `?v=<active theme name + css mtime>` into the shell's `<link>` at serve time, then serve the CSS `immutable, max-age=31536000`. A graft changes the token, so the URL changes, so pickup is instant AND there is zero revalidation in between (same trick SvelteKit uses for `_app/immutable`). Interim options considered and rejected for now: a short TTL (a grafted theme could appear one load late) and an ETag-only fix (saves a 23-byte body, not the round-trip). #perf #bonsai\n\n`#frontend`\n`TODO.md:L403`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 330
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "b7777d3e45943110",
      "line_num": 417,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#frontend"
      ],
      "text": "**startr.style adopted on the no-build pages (unversioned CDN, by decision) (Alexander, 2026-07-28: \"the unversioned for now, we'll be updating to versioned soon\"; `gauntlet_full` exit 0, both targets green \u2014 legacy 57/52 + 5 pending, nobuild 57/57). Loaded before `pages.css` so local rules win. The split is enforced rather than intended: props carry one-off layout on markup we AUTHOR; `pages.css` carries what repeats across generated rows, what the prop vocabulary cannot express (keyframes, `:focus-visible`, media queries), and anything that must survive the CDN being down. Verified in a real browser that the props actually apply. New gate `pages-cdn-outage.cy.ts`: with startr.style genuinely blocked, both pages still render every control, rows keep `display:grid` from the local sheet, and nothing overflows horizontally \u2014 an unversioned URL is a single point of failure by construction, and an air-gapped Rootstock is that case permanently. Two process notes worth more than the feature. (1) My first outage proof used a Playwright glob that matched NOTHING and passed while measuring the online page; caught only by printing the blocked-request count. The spec uses a regex and warns the next editor, since an aborted `<link>` still appears in `document.styleSheets` with its href. (2) The gate was then proved able to FAIL. EGRESS, recorded in `shell.py` rather than discovered later: this is the one place the product reaches off-machine on a normal page load. A stylesheet leaks far less than a script \u2014 no cross-origin cookies, cannot read the page \u2014 but it does tell startr.style that someone opened an admin page, and an air-gapped deployment gets unstyled chrome. Both stop being true with the versioned URL and SRI. NOT applied to ui-Sprig fragments; the validator refuses external references.**\n`#frontend`\n`TODO.md:L417`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 420
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "132a059d1d40724f",
      "line_num": 419,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#frontend"
      ],
      "text": "**The migration convention is written down (2026-07-28, `docs/no-build-surface-convention.md`). Two surfaces through the process \u2014 Sprigs 231 \u2192 157 (32%) and Diagnostics 813 \u2192 273 (67%, at parity, verified by the parity gate rather than claimed) \u2014 is enough evidence to stop re-deciding it per surface. The doc carries the ORDER of work (register \u2192 hook the legacy controls \u2192 guard-rail green on legacy \u2192 build \u2192 let the parity gate find what you missed \u2192 `e2e_both` \u2192 measure) and every design rule with the surface that paid for it. The rule that explains most of the gap between 32% and 67%: native HTML before custom widgets \u2014 `<details>` deleted the Svelte `Collapsible` and the 267-line how-to-fix modal outright. Two standing limits are recorded rather than footnoted: the parity gate is a coverage FLOOR, and a green suite is the weakest evidence on an interactive surface, so every surface gets a human pass via `scripts/manual-check.sh` before it takes over a route.**\n`#frontend`\n`TODO.md:L419`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 510
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "0840e893424385e2",
      "line_num": 421,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#frontend",
        "#bonsai"
      ],
      "text": "**Graft errors persist on the Sprig; panel messages became CSS toasts (Alexander, 2026-07-28: errors \"should also stay as an additional inline to the sprig that hit them\u2026 they stay visible even on page reload\", and the messages \"can be fixed to act as toasts by using a css animation to fade them out after a little bit also gluing it to the top of the window\"). The error belongs to the Sprig, not to the session or the clicker. `supervisor.record_error(name, message, phase)` writes into `state.json` beside `grafted` on the DATA VOLUME, so it outlives a reload, a restart, and a full container recreation \u2014 verified against a real recreation rather than asserted, since a durability claim tested only by reloading the tab is not a durability test. It is cleared by a successful graft or a successful prune and by nothing else, because a restart does not fix a missing dependency and so must not look like it did. Scope was Alexander's call \u2014 \"property of the sprig as it affects all the team using the Sage.is AI-UI instance\" \u2014 so `/catalog` carries `errors` and every operator sees the same record. The toast is the notification; the card is the record. Panel messages are now a CSS animation fixed to the top of the viewport, 6s for a success and 20s for an error, because an error names a cause you were not expecting and six seconds is not long enough to read one. Fixed positioning is the actual fix to what Alexander hit: the message rendered inside the panel, and on a long list it appeared off-screen. No script is involved \u2014 htmx swaps the WHOLE panel, so every message is a new element and the animation restarts by itself, which is the one place \"swap the whole panel\" pays a dividend nobody designed for.**\n`#frontend` `#bonsai`\n`TODO.md:L421`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 600
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "fd7d551cdd5ec2b5",
      "line_num": 431,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**All twelve panels converted to Jinja2 (2026-08-01; `e2e_both` exit 0 both targets \u2014 legacy 163 tests/131 passing/32 pending, nobuild 163/163 \u2014 plus `reload_gate` and `wizard_smoke` PASS; uncommitted, user commits). The spike's recommendation, carried out. Not one guard-rail spec was touched \u2014 that was the contract and it held for all twelve.**\n`TODO.md:L431`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 690
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "ede5d7116f324c6e",
      "line_num": 445,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Templating spike \u2014 Jinja2 on one panel, and the dev-loop claim holds (2026-07-31; `e2e_both` exit 0 both targets \u2014 legacy 163 tests/131 passing/32 pending, nobuild 163/163 \u2014 plus `reload_gate` and `wizard_smoke` PASS; uncommitted, user commits). Templating was rejected once on line count and reopened on Alexander's _\"We may lower this bar. as engines often come with other benefits.\"_ One panel converted, `welcome_panel.py`, so the decision on the other eleven rests on numbers.**\n`TODO.md:L445`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 780
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "bf8777cef32e93eb",
      "line_num": 468,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#frontend"
      ],
      "text": "**`/pages/` is a route index now, not the chat window (2026-07-31; `e2e_both` exit 0 both targets \u2014 legacy 163 tests/131 passing/32 pending, nobuild 163/163 \u2014 plus `reload_gate` and `wizard_smoke` PASS; uncommitted, user commits). Asked for as a dev affordance; built always-on and admin-only instead, because an index that exists only in a review container is one you cannot open on the instance you are actually debugging \u2014 and nothing is disclosed, since an admin can already reach all thirteen pages and until now had to know the URLs. It also closes a real confusion: `/pages/` used to answer with the SPA shell. Registered as BOTH `\"\"` and `\"/\"` rather than leaning on redirect-on-missing-slash, for exactly that reason \u2014 an unclaimed path here is answered by the app rather than redirected. Killed a duplication on the way in: the three whole-page surfaces carried heading, subheading and title as literals in each route body, and the index needed them a fourth time; they live once in `_PAGES` now. The nine setup panels come from `_SETUP_ORDER`, so the list is in wizard order because the sequence is the meaning. A dev banner appears only when `PAGES_RELOAD_DIRS` is on. Fragment endpoints and the reloader's event stream are left off deliberately \u2014 one returns markup with no shell, the other never finishes. The gate's real assertion is that every entry GOES somewhere, because a dead link on an index is worse than no index; proved able to fail by pointing the setup links at a typo, and the useful half was NOT the status check, since a dead `/pages/` link still returns 200. Built and verified without a single rebuild \u2014 the reloader picked up a brand-new route from a live edit.**\n`#frontend`\n`TODO.md:L468`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 870
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "647806e45e35b6aa",
      "line_num": 470,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#frontend",
        "#dx"
      ],
      "text": "**The no-build pages reload themselves, and the browser follows (2026-07-31; `e2e_both` exit 0 both targets \u2014 legacy 159 tests/131 passing/28 pending, nobuild 159/159 \u2014 plus `wizard_smoke` PASS and the new `make reload_gate` PASS; uncommitted, user commits). Third time a capability existed and was missing from the one place a human works: `dev.sh` has run `uvicorn --reload` for years, and `start.sh`, which `manual-check.sh` boots, did not. `PAGES_RELOAD_DIRS` is the one switch for both halves \u2014 `start.sh` branches on it (a branch, not extra flags, because uvicorn refuses `--reload` with `--workers`), and the pages shell reads the same value to decide whether to serve the browser-refresh island, so the two cannot drift apart. The presence of a VALUE is the enable; there is no companion boolean to be set to on-while-watching-nothing. `--log-level info`, not `warning`, and that is load-bearing: the reloader announces a restart at INFO, so at `warning` it restarts in total silence, which reads as \"my edit did nothing\" \u2014 the exact failure the feature removes. The browser half costs no dependency: a uvicorn restart drops every open connection and `EventSource` reconnects by itself, so THE RESTART IS THE SIGNAL and nothing watches `.py` twice. Assets are the opposite case and do need a watcher, because `/pages/_assets` is served from disk: `watchfiles.awatch` pushes an `assets` event and the island re-stamps same-origin stylesheet hrefs without reloading, so you can restyle a panel without losing your scroll or closing the dialog you are looking at. Measured, after a whole session of saying \"~3s\": a panel edit is served 2.8 seconds after saving, a mode switch is 7, a fresh seeded boot is 8. Switching is `make review` / `review_live` / `review_rebuild`; the friction was never typing the flag, but that a bind mount cannot be added to a running container and teardown deleted the data volume, so `REUSE_DATA=1` keeps it. The gates deliberately have no such switch, said in the Makefile beside the targets that do. Two gates, because one could not reach both states: `pages-dev-reload.cy.ts` covers the OFF state that every shipped instance runs, and `scripts/gates/dev-reload/run-gate.sh` boots its own container with the flag and edits a COPY of `pages/`. Three things the gates turned up. (1) The off-state spec first asserted a 404 and went red at 200 \u2014 nothing under `/pages/` can 404, so it checks the observable fact (no `text/event-stream`) and says why the obvious version is wrong. (2) Proving the gate could fail put the app in a RESTART LOOP and the gate hung for ten minutes instead of reporting, so every curl carries `--max-time` \u2014 a gate that hangs on a broken product has the same defect as one that passes on it. (3) `Diagnostics.svelte` hardcoded its three `boot_status` keys while the no-build page enumerates whatever arrives, so a new check rendered on one side and silently nowhere on the other; fixed by enumerating from the response, which kills the class.**\n`#frontend` `#dx`\n`TODO.md:L470`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 960
    },
    {
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "c39491f0fa19a9b0",
      "line_num": 472,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**`manual-check.sh` grew `LIVE=1` \u2014 looking at a no-build page no longer costs a build (2026-07-31; Alexander: _\"Souldn't we work in such a way the no build is needed for our no build changes???\"_). Fair, and the gap was narrower than it looked: `dev_run` already bind-mounts `app/backend/`, so the capability existed \u2014 it was missing from the ONE script whose entire job is letting a human look at these surfaces. `manual-check.sh` booted the baked image with no source mount, so a one-line style tweak on a server-rendered page meant rebuilding a 619 MB image, against surfaces whose whole selling point is that there is no build step. `LIVE=1` now mounts `sage_is_ai/pages/` over the image's copy. Both halves measured rather than assumed, because they reload differently and guessing wrong reads as \"my edit did nothing\": `pages/assets/*.css` and `*.js` need only a REFRESH (the `/pages/_assets` `StaticFiles` mount reads from disk per request \u2014 proved by appending a marker and serving it with no rebuild); `pages/*.py` needs `docker restart sage-manual`, about 3 seconds, because the panel modules are imported once at boot and that start path has no `--reload` (proved 0 before the restart, 1 after). Both are printed in the walkthrough so nobody has to find out. OFF by default and that is deliberate: Phase S made the human pass a standing condition because a green suite is the weakest evidence on an interactive surface, and a review of your working tree is not a review of the artifact you ship. Default when deciding whether it ships; `LIVE=1` while still changing it. Narrower than `dev_run`'s whole-backend mount on purpose \u2014 only `pages/` is shadowed, so everything the panels call into is still baked. #frontend #dx <!-- codespell:ignore -->**\n`TODO.md:L472`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 1050
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 475,
          "tags": [
            "#frontend"
          ],
          "text": "**Diagnostics migrated \u2014 813 \u2192 273 lines, 67% at parity** (2026-07-28, commit `9a2bae5`). The second fragment surface, and the one that made the convention worth writing down: it more than doubled the Sprigs panel's 32% cut, and the difference is almost entirely **native HTML over custom widgets** \u2014 `<details>` replaced the Svelte `Collapsible` and deleted the 267-line how-to-fix modal outright. `fixRegistry.json` and `commandLibrary.json` are now data BOTH implementations read, so the 40 remediation steps exist once instead of being transcribed into Python. Per-row re-probe posts to a fragment route that calls `probe_endpoint` directly \u2014 that handler refuses any URL not currently configured, so reimplementing it to save lines would have routed around the SSRF defence. Detail and the parity-gate story in the two entries above."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 476,
          "tags": [
            "#frontend"
          ],
          "text": "**Theme/branding migrated \u2014 288 \u2192 200 lines, 31%, with ZERO new CSS** (2026-07-29; `e2e_both` green both targets, legacy 67 tests/60 passing/7 pending, nobuild 67/67; uncommitted, user commits). The third fragment surface and the FIRST that is only a form \u2014 the shape the plan claimed htmx deletes the most code on. `/pages/admin/branding` renders seven fields filled in, saves through `routers/configs.set_branding` directly, and swaps the whole panel back. **The course correction is the real lesson.** The first version hand-wrote 99 lines in `pages.css` and landed at 288 \u2192 265, an 8% cut, reported honestly as weak. Alexander made the call the number was pointing at \u2014 *\"we should be using the inline props costing zero lines across the board\"*, then *\"avoid custom classes when possible and avoid almost all custom elements, HTML has our back\"* \u2014 and converting to props deleted the stylesheet outright, taking the cut from **8% to 31%**, in line with Sprigs' 32%. **The old rule was wrong on its own terms**: `pages.css` claimed a class beats props on GENERATED markup, but the props are written ONCE in the render helper and repeated by the generator, so five fields share one `_INPUT_S` string. That header comment is corrected in place. **Second correction, same direction**: `<fieldset>`/`<legend>` for the field groups, `<p>`/`<label>`/`<small>` per row, `<output>` for the preview \u2014 the rendered page contains **zero class attributes and zero ARIA attributes**. The one class left is the self-fading toast, because `@keyframes` is what the prop vocabulary genuinely cannot express, and that is the test for when a class is still right. **One script added, deliberately**: `pages/assets/color-pair.js`, 19 lines, because `<input type=\"color\">` cannot express \"unset\" and unset is meaningful here. A FILE, not two `oninput` attributes, since the diagnostics page tells operators to set a CSP and inline handlers are the first thing that breaks; delegated from the document, so it survives htmx swapping the form out. **A deliberate behaviour difference**: the Svelte preview updates on every keystroke, the fragment's reflects SAVED branding \u2014 reproducing live typing means a round-trip per keystroke or a client-side copy of the form state, the second being the duplication this migration deletes. Written into the spec and the UX-review item rather than left to be discovered. **Process**: registered first; `branding` added to the `RESTORED_CONFIG` leak guard BEFORE any spec mutated it, since it sets the `--primary` the whole UI cascades from; guard-rail green against Svelte before code moved. **The parity gate passed on the first run, which is when it deserves least trust**, so it was proved able to fail by deleting the save hook."
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 477,
          "tags": [
            "#frontend"
          ],
          "text": "**Setup wizard CUT OVER \u2014 1,816 \u2192 1,294 lines, 29%, and the modal is deleted** (2026-07-31; `e2e_both` exit 0 both targets \u2014 legacy 155 tests/131 passing/24 pending, nobuild 155/155 \u2014 and `wizard_smoke` PASS; uncommitted, user commits). The last Phase 2 surface and the only one that was never fragment-shaped: nine panels with real client state between them. `ChangesAndSetupModal.svelte` and its nine step components \u2014 **2,132 raw lines, 1,816 of code** \u2014 are GONE. What shows those panels now is `SetupDialog.svelte`, **168 lines**, which fetches one of the nine routes, lifts the panel out of the response and puts it in a native `<dialog>`. Nothing in it renders a panel; markup, defaults, validation and every save path live on the server, in one language. **A native `<dialog>` rather than `Modal.svelte`, because the element already does four things that component hand-rolls**: Escape to close, focus trapped inside, everything behind it inert, and a `::backdrop` needing no fixed-position overlay. **Extracted, not framed** \u2014 the panel is lifted into the app's own document rather than shown in an iframe, so the nine wizard specs keep reaching their hooks with a plain `cy.get`, and an operator's CSP has no say in whether the wizard works. **The cost of sharing a document is a stylesheet, measured rather than assumed**: the nine panels contain zero `class` attributes, so what `pages.css` still owned for them is exactly `var(--line)` and three pager rules \u2014 four rules scoped to the dialog cover it. Loading the sheet itself would have imported its `:root { color-scheme: light dark }` and handed every form control in the app to the OS theme. **How a flow ENDS is a redirect, not a flag**: the two finish routes 303 to `/`, and the host closes when a response lands outside `/pages/`. One mechanism serves both readers \u2014 with JavaScript the dialog closes, without it the browser lands back in the app. **A regression this nearly shipped, caught by reading rather than by a gate**: Settings \u2192 About \u2192 \"See what's new\" is a control EVERY user has, and pointing it at an admin route would have turned a working button into a 403 for everyone who is not an admin. Fixed with `require_page_user` and `/pages/changelog`, sharing the renderer through a `base` parameter so a second copy cannot drift. **The parity gate found a real defect in this work.** The first version left the `<dialog>` mounted and closed on every page; a closed dialog still renders its children, so three hooks appeared on EVERY SvelteKit page and `surface-parity` went red naming all three. **Test estate changed deliberately**: the nine wizard entries are OUT of `surfaces.ts`, because with one implementation left that gate would compare a route against itself; `openSetupPanel()` replaces them; every `isNoBuild()` skip in the wizard specs is gone, since a guard that never skips hides the only implementation from half the runs; and `pages-cdn-outage.cy.ts` re-lists the nine routes explicitly, because it iterated the registry and would have silently dropped them. **`setup-dialog.cy.ts` took their place**, nine tests on the only code the cut-over added. **All four new gates proved able to fail before being trusted.** Escape is deliberately NOT asserted: Cypress dispatches synthetic key events a browser does not treat as real, so the close button \u2014 which is ours \u2014 is asserted instead. **Three filed bugs closed by deletion.**"
        },
        {
          "checked": true,
          "line_num": 478,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "**Locale negotiation \u2014 RESOLVED 2026-07-31, and it was the standing blocker on every no-build surface** (surfaced 2026-07-28 building `pages/i18n.py`). Server-rendered pages read `en-US` and nothing else, because the reader's language lives in `localStorage` where no server can see it \u2014 so the moment one of them BECAME a real route, every non-English operator silently lost their language. **Chosen: the URL, not a cookie.** `locale_for()` resolves `?lang=` first and falls back to negotiating `Accept-Language` against the shipped catalogs; the image now carries all 56 rather than `en-US` alone (5 MB against 604); and every link, form action and redirect carries the parameter onward through one helper, so a reader cannot lose their language by pressing Next. A cookie would have forced `Vary: Cookie`, and the auth cookie rides in the same header, so nothing would ever share a cache entry \u2014 which forecloses the parent plan's third leg. **What made it safe to apply broadly**: the keys ARE the English text, so an untranslated key renders as English rather than as a blank. That needed one guard \u2014 1,511 of the 1,538 `en-US` entries are stored as `\"\"`, so without treating empty as unresolved, wrapping a panel string would have blanked it for every English reader and read as a CSS bug. Traversal closed at both layers before the value ever came from a request, and proved by removing each independently. 67 call sites across nine panels; `pages-locale.cy.ts` 8/8, including that no response carries `Vary: Cookie`. **Still open**: `diagnostics_panel.py` renders English only \u2014 filed under Bugs."
        }
      ],
      "file_path": "TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "8e10c3e387220b47",
      "line_num": 474,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Phase 2 \u2014 ripe surfaces COMPLETE: theme/branding, wizard, diagnostics. All three migrated, and the wizard is the first surface where the SvelteKit side was actually DELETED rather than left running beside it \u2014 which is what turns the migration's line saving from a projection into a fact.**\n- [x] **Diagnostics migrated \u2014 813 \u2192 273 lines, 67% at parity** (2026-07-28, commit `9a2bae5`). The second fragment surface, and the one that made the convention worth writing down: it more than doubled the Sprigs panel's 32% cut, and the difference is almost entirely **native HTML over custom widgets** \u2014 `<details>` replaced the Svelte `Collapsible` and deleted the 267-line how-to-fix modal outright. `fixRegistry.json` and `commandLibrary.json` are now data BOTH implementations read, so the 40 remediation steps exist once instead of being transcribed into Python. Per-row re-probe posts to a fragment route that calls `probe_endpoint` directly \u2014 that handler refuses any URL not currently configured, so reimplementing it to save lines would have routed around the SSRF defence. Detail and the parity-gate story in the two entries above. #frontend\n- [x] **Theme/branding migrated \u2014 288 \u2192 200 lines, 31%, with ZERO new CSS** (2026-07-29; `e2e_both` green both targets, legacy 67 tests/60 passing/7 pending, nobuild 67/67; uncommitted, user commits). The third fragment surface and the FIRST that is only a form \u2014 the shape the plan claimed htmx deletes the most code on. `/pages/admin/branding` renders seven fields filled in, saves through `routers/configs.set_branding` directly, and swaps the whole panel back. **The course correction is the real lesson.** The first version hand-wrote 99 lines in `pages.css` and landed at 288 \u2192 265, an 8% cut, reported honestly as weak. Alexander made the call the number was pointing at \u2014 *\"we should be using the inline props costing zero lines across the board\"*, then *\"avoid custom classes when possible and avoid almost all custom elements, HTML has our back\"* \u2014 and converting to props deleted the stylesheet outright, taking the cut from **8% to 31%**, in line with Sprigs' 32%. **The old rule was wrong on its own terms**: `pages.css` claimed a class beats props on GENERATED markup, but the props are written ONCE in the render helper and repeated by the generator, so five fields share one `_INPUT_S` string. That header comment is corrected in place. **Second correction, same direction**: `<fieldset>`/`<legend>` for the field groups, `<p>`/`<label>`/`<small>` per row, `<output>` for the preview \u2014 the rendered page contains **zero class attributes and zero ARIA attributes**. The one class left is the self-fading toast, because `@keyframes` is what the prop vocabulary genuinely cannot express, and that is the test for when a class is still right. **One script added, deliberately**: `pages/assets/color-pair.js`, 19 lines, because `<input type=\"color\">` cannot express \"unset\" and unset is meaningful here. A FILE, not two `oninput` attributes, since the diagnostics page tells operators to set a CSP and inline handlers are the first thing that breaks; delegated from the document, so it survives htmx swapping the form out. **A deliberate behaviour difference**: the Svelte preview updates on every keystroke, the fragment's reflects SAVED branding \u2014 reproducing live typing means a round-trip per keystroke or a client-side copy of the form state, the second being the duplication this migration deletes. Written into the spec and the UX-review item rather than left to be discovered. **Process**: registered first; `branding` added to the `RESTORED_CONFIG` leak guard BEFORE any spec mutated it, since it sets the `--primary` the whole UI cascades from; guard-rail green against Svelte before code moved. **The parity gate passed on the first run, which is when it deserves least trust**, so it was proved able to fail by deleting the save hook. #frontend\n- [x] **Setup wizard CUT OVER \u2014 1,816 \u2192 1,294 lines, 29%, and the modal is deleted** (2026-07-31; `e2e_both` exit 0 both targets \u2014 legacy 155 tests/131 passing/24 pending, nobuild 155/155 \u2014 and `wizard_smoke` PASS; uncommitted, user commits). The last Phase 2 surface and the only one that was never fragment-shaped: nine panels with real client state between them. `ChangesAndSetupModal.svelte` and its nine step components \u2014 **2,132 raw lines, 1,816 of code** \u2014 are GONE. What shows those panels now is `SetupDialog.svelte`, **168 lines**, which fetches one of the nine routes, lifts the panel out of the response and puts it in a native `<dialog>`. Nothing in it renders a panel; markup, defaults, validation and every save path live on the server, in one language. **A native `<dialog>` rather than `Modal.svelte`, because the element already does four things that component hand-rolls**: Escape to close, focus trapped inside, everything behind it inert, and a `::backdrop` needing no fixed-position overlay. **Extracted, not framed** \u2014 the panel is lifted into the app's own document rather than shown in an iframe, so the nine wizard specs keep reaching their hooks with a plain `cy.get`, and an operator's CSP has no say in whether the wizard works. **The cost of sharing a document is a stylesheet, measured rather than assumed**: the nine panels contain zero `class` attributes, so what `pages.css` still owned for them is exactly `var(--line)` and three pager rules \u2014 four rules scoped to the dialog cover it. Loading the sheet itself would have imported its `:root { color-scheme: light dark }` and handed every form control in the app to the OS theme. **How a flow ENDS is a redirect, not a flag**: the two finish routes 303 to `/`, and the host closes when a response lands outside `/pages/`. One mechanism serves both readers \u2014 with JavaScript the dialog closes, without it the browser lands back in the app. **A regression this nearly shipped, caught by reading rather than by a gate**: Settings \u2192 About \u2192 \"See what's new\" is a control EVERY user has, and pointing it at an admin route would have turned a working button into a 403 for everyone who is not an admin. Fixed with `require_page_user` and `/pages/changelog`, sharing the renderer through a `base` parameter so a second copy cannot drift. **The parity gate found a real defect in this work.** The first version left the `<dialog>` mounted and closed on every page; a closed dialog still renders its children, so three hooks appeared on EVERY SvelteKit page and `surface-parity` went red naming all three. **Test estate changed deliberately**: the nine wizard entries are OUT of `surfaces.ts`, because with one implementation left that gate would compare a route against itself; `openSetupPanel()` replaces them; every `isNoBuild()` skip in the wizard specs is gone, since a guard that never skips hides the only implementation from half the runs; and `pages-cdn-outage.cy.ts` re-lists the nine routes explicitly, because it iterated the registry and would have silently dropped them. **`setup-dialog.cy.ts` took their place**, nine tests on the only code the cut-over added. **All four new gates proved able to fail before being trusted.** Escape is deliberately NOT asserted: Cypress dispatches synthetic key events a browser does not treat as real, so the close button \u2014 which is ours \u2014 is asserted instead. **Three filed bugs closed by deletion.** #frontend\n- [x] **Locale negotiation \u2014 RESOLVED 2026-07-31, and it was the standing blocker on every no-build surface** (surfaced 2026-07-28 building `pages/i18n.py`). Server-rendered pages read `en-US` and nothing else, because the reader's language lives in `localStorage` where no server can see it \u2014 so the moment one of them BECAME a real route, every non-English operator silently lost their language. **Chosen: the URL, not a cookie.** `locale_for()` resolves `?lang=` first and falls back to negotiating `Accept-Language` against the shipped catalogs; the image now carries all 56 rather than `en-US` alone (5 MB against 604); and every link, form action and redirect carries the parameter onward through one helper, so a reader cannot lose their language by pressing Next. A cookie would have forced `Vary: Cookie`, and the auth cookie rides in the same header, so nothing would ever share a cache entry \u2014 which forecloses the parent plan's third leg. **What made it safe to apply broadly**: the keys ARE the English text, so an untranslated key renders as English rather than as a blank. That needed one guard \u2014 1,511 of the 1,538 `en-US` entries are stored as `\"\"`, so without treating empty as unresolved, wrapping a panel string would have blanked it for every English reader and read as a CSS bug. Traversal closed at both layers before the value ever came from a request, and proved by removing each independently. 67 call sites across nine panels; `pages-locale.cy.ts` 8/8, including that no response carries `Vary: Cookie`. **Still open**: `diagnostics_panel.py` renders English only \u2014 filed under Bugs.\n\n`TODO.md:L474`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 1140
    }
  ]
}